WEBVTT

1
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:29.538
<i>Will you enter, mystery
challenger, and sign in, please?</i>

2
00:00:31.114 --> 00:00:32.832
<i>Are you in the movies?</i>

3
00:00:34.993 --> 00:00:36.245
No.

4
00:00:36.370 --> 00:00:38.919
<i>One down, nine to go. Mr. Gabe].</i>

5
00:00:39.039 --> 00:00:40.666
<i>Are you an athlete?</i>

6
00:00:40.791 --> 00:00:42.714
Mm... Yes.

7
00:00:42.834 --> 00:00:43.960
<i>"MAN". Miss Francis.</i>

8
00:00:44.086 --> 00:00:46.714
<i>Are you a golfer,
mystery guest?</i>

9
00:00:46.838 --> 00:00:47.838
No.

10
00:00:47.881 --> 00:00:50.805
<i>That's five down and five to go.
Mr Gabel.</i>

11
00:00:52.803 --> 00:00:54.851
<i>Are you, uh, a boxer?</i>

12
00:00:56.807 --> 00:00:59.276
<i>- Yes.
- Miss Francis.</i>

13
00:00:59.393 --> 00:01:01.395
<i>Boy, that sure isn't the voice fur it,
is it?</i>

14
00:01:03.897 --> 00:01:06.741
<i>Let me see now.
What could that be?</i>

15
00:01:06.858 --> 00:01:10.203
<i>Are you a fellow who was named
after a chap</i>

16
00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:14.120
<i>that they brought the mountains to?
By the name of Muhammad?</i>

17
00:01:15.158 --> 00:01:16.455
<i>- Are you Cassius Clay?
- Well...</i>

18
00:01:16.577 --> 00:01:17.703
Yes!

19
00:01:30.215 --> 00:01:31.262
Friday.

20
00:01:34.636 --> 00:01:36.980
<i>- Yes?
- Assalamu alaikum, Grandma!</i>

21
00:01:37.097 --> 00:01:39.600
<i>- Waiaikum assalam!
- What y'all doing?</i>

22
00:01:39.725 --> 00:01:41.568
<i>Downstairs watching television.</i>

23
00:01:41.685 --> 00:01:43.733
<i>- Yeah? Where's May May?
- Downstairs.</i>

24
00:01:43.854 --> 00:01:45.494
<i>Don't tell her.
Put her on the phone.</i>

25
00:01:45.522 --> 00:01:47.900
<i>OK. May May!</i>

26
00:01:50.444 --> 00:01:53.072
<i>- Yes?
- Assalamu alaikum!</i>

27
00:01:53.196 --> 00:01:56.826
- Walaikum assalam, Daddy!
- How's my daughter?

28
00:01:56.950 --> 00:02:00.170
<i>Fine. We went to go
see Mama Bird.</i>

29
00:02:00.287 --> 00:02:04.087
<i>Good. I heard that.
Did you... were you happy to see her?</i>

30
00:02:04.207 --> 00:02:06.426
<i>- Yes.
- ALP. I was just getting dressed.</i>

31
00:02:06.543 --> 00:02:10.389
<i>I'm getting ready to go look
at another place like Deer Lake.</i>

32
00:02:10.505 --> 00:02:12.052
- You are?
- Yes.

33
00:02:12.174 --> 00:02:18.477
<i>It might be possible
that if I like it, I might fight again.</i>

34
00:02:19.848 --> 00:02:23.603
<i>No! Don't fight again, please.</i>

35
00:02:23.727 --> 00:02:27.903
<i>Take the title back four times.
Can you imagine?</i>

36
00:02:28.023 --> 00:02:31.493
<i>Don't fight again.
You're getting old.</i>

37
00:02:33.403 --> 00:02:35.952
<i>- I'm getting what?
- Old.</i>

38
00:02:36.073 --> 00:02:39.748
<i>ALP. Too old? Haw old is your daddy?</i>

39
00:02:39.868 --> 00:02:44.374
<i>- How old? Like, 37?
- That's right.</i>

40
00:02:46.833 --> 00:02:52.215
<i>Angelo Dundee is in the ring,
the veteran trainer of Muhammad Ali</i>

41
00:02:52.339 --> 00:02:54.967
<i>since the first couple of fights
of his career.</i>

42
00:02:57.886 --> 00:03:04.189
<i>The crowd is starting to chant,
"Ali. Ali." Like old times.</i>

43
00:03:07.396 --> 00:03:12.152
<i>Ali is six feet three,
but Trevor Berbick is not small.</i>

44
00:03:12.275 --> 00:03:14.027
<i>He's six feet two and a half.</i>

45
00:03:16.113 --> 00:03:20.118
<i>Ali is almost 40 years old.
Trevor Berbick is 27.</i>

46
00:03:22.494 --> 00:03:23.962
<i>Here he comes.</i>

47
00:03:25.997 --> 00:03:27.965
<i>What a production this has
turned out to be.</i>

48
00:03:38.260 --> 00:03:40.354
<i>I knew what I wanted to do
at the age of 12 years old,</i>

49
00:03:40.470 --> 00:03:43.974
<i>and every thing has a purpose in life
and every man has a purpose.</i>

50
00:03:44.099 --> 00:03:48.149
<i>And it's the knowing of that purpose
which enables every soul to fulfill it.</i>

51
00:03:48.270 --> 00:03:53.572
<i>And he who knows his life purpose,
then he knows exactly where he's going.</i>

52
00:03:53.692 --> 00:03:55.786
<i>I'm not conceited, Fm just convinced.</i>

53
00:04:28.602 --> 00:04:31.526
<i>- My buddy, Muhammad Ali.
- How's the weather?</i>

54
00:04:31.646 --> 00:04:33.398
<i>- Huh?
- How's the weather?</i>

55
00:04:33.523 --> 00:04:36.402
<i>Beautiful here. Beautiful here.</i>

56
00:04:49.247 --> 00:04:53.468
My name is Gene Kilroy. I'm the former
business manager of Muhammad Ali.

57
00:04:53.585 --> 00:04:57.590
The greatest fighter of all times.
The greatest friend.

58
00:04:57.714 --> 00:05:01.264
The greatest guy.
I learned a lot from him.

59
00:05:02.135 --> 00:05:06.015
Yeah, man, I step in town, ljump off
a plane and in my cool white suit.

60
00:05:12.521 --> 00:05:15.240
, brothers!

61
00:05:18.193 --> 00:05:21.072
Boy, it was better than a ringside seat.

62
00:05:21.196 --> 00:05:25.417
You know, a ringside seat,
you gotta go when the fight's over.

63
00:05:25.534 --> 00:05:29.255
To be part of the victory
and part of the defeat,

64
00:05:29.371 --> 00:05:33.251
you know, that was a special thing.
You know, be right there.

65
00:05:33.375 --> 00:05:37.175
I wanna introduce a long-time
adviser of the champ, Gene Kilroy,

66
00:05:37.295 --> 00:05:39.639
who is our spokesperson today.
Gene.

67
00:05:41.800 --> 00:05:43.097
It's nice to be here.

68
00:05:43.218 --> 00:05:47.644
Looking back over my life, uh,
somebody asked me one time,

69
00:05:47.764 --> 00:05:50.438
uh, "Do you have any fear?"

70
00:05:50.559 --> 00:05:56.692
I said, "The only fear I have is that
some day my mother's gonna wake me up"

71
00:05:56.815 --> 00:06:00.445
and say 'It's time to go to school',
that my life has been a dream."

72
00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:06.075
With Muhammad Ali,
I've been blessed to be around that man.

73
00:06:06.199 --> 00:06:11.581
There are multi-millionaires
who would have given a million dollars

74
00:06:11.705 --> 00:06:14.458
to be with Ali
every day like I've been with him.

75
00:06:17.919 --> 00:06:22.971
My first encounter with Muhammad
Ali was the 1960 Olympics in Rome.

76
00:06:23.091 --> 00:06:25.640
He was like
the Mayor of Olympic Village.

77
00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:28.809
He wears a gold medal around,
everybody wanted to be around him.

78
00:06:30.015 --> 00:06:33.110
Everybody, everybody wanted to meet him.

79
00:06:33.226 --> 00:06:35.695
Tell me how you came
to get such a Roman name as that.

80
00:06:35.812 --> 00:06:39.942
Well, as I understand,
I'm Cassius Marcellus Clay the Sixth

81
00:06:40.066 --> 00:06:42.364
and my great-great-grandfather
was a Kentucky slave

82
00:06:42.485 --> 00:06:44.533
and he was named
after some great Kentuckian.

83
00:06:44.654 --> 00:06:47.874
Cassius Marcellus Clay is
a great name in Kentucky.

84
00:06:47.991 --> 00:06:52.497
And, uh, really, where he was from
and where it was all originated,

85
00:06:52.621 --> 00:06:56.000
I couldn't tell you, but since I've
reached a little fame in boxing,

86
00:06:56.124 --> 00:07:00.095
most people want to know where I'm from
and, uh, where did I get that name.

87
00:07:00.211 --> 00:07:01.929
But really,
I haven't really checked on it,

88
00:07:02.047 --> 00:07:03.847
so I see that I'm gonna have
to go look and...

89
00:07:03.965 --> 00:07:05.005
You'll have to look it up.

90
00:07:05.050 --> 00:07:07.290
See what it's all about,
now that I'm getting interviews.

91
00:07:12.766 --> 00:07:14.313
<i>- Hey, Gene.
- Yeah.</i>

92
00:07:14.434 --> 00:07:17.358
<i>I heard</i> a <i>little story you might like.
Goes like this.</i>

93
00:07:33.203 --> 00:07:37.049
As a boy,
I grew up in Louisville, Kentucky.

94
00:07:37.165 --> 00:07:41.545
In the state of Kentucky that has
pretty horses and fast women.

95
00:07:44.506 --> 00:07:46.804
I come from a really loving,
close family.

96
00:07:46.925 --> 00:07:48.973
My dad and ma were Christian people.

97
00:07:49.094 --> 00:07:51.222
Very hardworking
and good, decent people.

98
00:07:51.346 --> 00:07:53.440
My dad was a commercial artist.

99
00:07:53.556 --> 00:07:55.796
My dad was the best sign painter
in Louisville, Kentucky.

100
00:07:55.892 --> 00:07:59.988
Way back in a little one-horse town
called Louisville, Kentucky,

101
00:08:00.105 --> 00:08:05.862
back in 1941, Mr Cassius Clay married
Mrs Odessa Grady

102
00:08:05.986 --> 00:08:10.287
who later became Mrs Cassius Clay
and they had a little son.

103
00:08:10.407 --> 00:08:12.409
This is Mrs Clay.

104
00:08:13.368 --> 00:08:16.963
And this is my daddy.
Mr Cassius Clay Senior.

105
00:08:17.956 --> 00:08:19.708
- Say hello.
- Hello. Hello.

106
00:08:19.833 --> 00:08:21.793
- We're very proud of him, lady.
- Thank you.

107
00:08:31.886 --> 00:08:36.266
I was born in 1943.
Muhammad was born in 1942.

108
00:08:36.391 --> 00:08:38.439
We were 18 months apart. We had fun.

109
00:08:38.560 --> 00:08:40.904
In those days he was
known as Cassius Clay.

110
00:08:41.021 --> 00:08:42.773
He was loquacious, he was cocky.

111
00:08:42.897 --> 00:08:47.277
He's always been different.
He was born for greatness.

112
00:08:52.949 --> 00:08:57.796
Today we're going to my old home site
down at 3302 Grant Avenue,

113
00:08:57.912 --> 00:08:59.164
here in Louisville, Kentucky.

114
00:08:59.289 --> 00:09:02.463
The house where Muhammad Ali
and myself was raised.

115
00:09:02.584 --> 00:09:05.258
We shared a room, and that was one
of the most happiest times of my life.

116
00:09:05.378 --> 00:09:08.928
He was funny. He was funny. And
everything he did, I would go with him.

117
00:09:09.049 --> 00:09:11.268
The running, the boxing, everything.

118
00:09:11.384 --> 00:09:14.228
I was just his best companion
in those days. I was his best friend.

119
00:09:14.345 --> 00:09:19.442
We were that close, like salt
and pepper. Like salt and pepper.

120
00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:26.734
And it all begins here.
From the root to the fruit.

121
00:09:42.707 --> 00:09:46.803
<i>Maryum, tell me something.
When I started boxing, how old was I?</i>

122
00:09:48.129 --> 00:09:49.426
<i>I was 12 years old.</i>

123
00:09:49.547 --> 00:09:50.924
You were 12?!

124
00:09:51.049 --> 00:09:53.848
<i>- Yeah.
- I thought you was younger.</i>

125
00:09:53.968 --> 00:09:56.812
<i>- Twelve.
- 'Cause somebody stole your bike.</i>

126
00:09:56.930 --> 00:09:58.682
<i>Yeah, thatb when I was 12.</i>

127
00:10:08.525 --> 00:10:12.871
It was on a Monday night
in Louisville, 1953.

128
00:10:12.987 --> 00:10:15.991
We both went to a home show. Every year
they had, like, a big carnival.

129
00:10:16.116 --> 00:10:19.086
We had rode our bicycle
we got for Christmas up there.

130
00:10:19.202 --> 00:10:20.920
And when we came out
of the home show,

131
00:10:21.037 --> 00:10:22.835
our bicycles had got stolen
so we cried.

132
00:10:22.956 --> 00:10:24.924
We were little boys.
We started crying.

133
00:10:25.041 --> 00:10:27.214
So we asked somebody coming out
of the building.

134
00:10:27.335 --> 00:10:30.930
"Please tell us where we could find
a policeman to report our bikes stolen."

135
00:10:31.047 --> 00:10:34.301
And the gentleman said, "Go downstairs
and there's a policeman downstairs."

136
00:10:34.425 --> 00:10:36.785
So we went down a long flight of steps.
It was a boxing gym.

137
00:10:36.803 --> 00:10:40.524
The policeman was in the corridor
talking to some kids.

138
00:10:40.640 --> 00:10:42.642
He was all hostile
and wanted to whip somebody.

139
00:10:42.767 --> 00:10:46.067
So I said,
"You never fight if you don't know how."

140
00:10:46.187 --> 00:10:48.815
I said, "Has anybody ever taught you
how to fight?" and he said, "No."

141
00:10:48.940 --> 00:10:51.284
I said, "You should come down here
and learn how to fight first"

142
00:10:51.401 --> 00:10:53.278
before you starting picking fights."

143
00:10:53.403 --> 00:10:54.700
So that was the beginning of it.

144
00:10:54.821 --> 00:10:57.449
He started coming to the gym
and he was a very religious trainer.

145
00:10:57.574 --> 00:11:00.293
And so he was there when I got there,
and he was there when I left.

146
00:11:00.410 --> 00:11:05.758
He <i>was</i> 12 years old and weighed <i>87</i>
pounds. That's how he got started.

147
00:11:09.544 --> 00:11:13.174
<i>Cassius Clay of Chicago
challenges Gary Joyce,</i>

148
00:11:13.298 --> 00:11:15.175
<i>the Eastern Heavyweight champ.</i>

149
00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.928
<i>And Joyce is in trouble
from the first round on.</i>

150
00:11:19.137 --> 00:11:21.640
When he discovered boxing,
he was smart.

151
00:11:21.764 --> 00:11:25.018
He said, "if I can dive, move
from side to side in the boxing ring",

152
00:11:25.143 --> 00:11:26.343
I know I'm not gonna get hit."

153
00:11:26.394 --> 00:11:28.434
So he said, "Rudy,
let's go to the back of the alley"

154
00:11:28.521 --> 00:11:30.523
and pick up rocks
and throw the rocks at me."

155
00:11:30.648 --> 00:11:33.071
"See if I can dodge the rocks."
So he would...

156
00:11:33.193 --> 00:11:36.868
I'd throw the rocks at him. The rocks
would not hit him and go by him.

157
00:11:36.988 --> 00:11:39.662
He would dodge the rocks,
improve his reflexes as a boxer.

158
00:11:39.782 --> 00:11:42.285
And sure enough,
when he got in the boxing ring,

159
00:11:42.410 --> 00:11:46.665
he would dodge those punches.
He was a genius.

160
00:11:54.088 --> 00:11:57.433
Back in the days when Muhammad
and I was young kids,

161
00:11:57.550 --> 00:12:00.099
he would tell the children
in our neighborhood his destiny,

162
00:12:00.220 --> 00:12:01.847
how great he would be.

163
00:12:01.971 --> 00:12:03.644
Most of the kids would laugh
at Muhammad.

164
00:12:03.765 --> 00:12:06.143
Say, "Ha, ha, ha, Cassius,
you can't do that."

165
00:12:06.267 --> 00:12:09.020
"That's too much for you to do.
You can't be that great."

166
00:12:10.188 --> 00:12:11.988
When you're a kid,
you always bet some fellas,

167
00:12:12.065 --> 00:12:13.362
"I'm gonna be champion one day,

168
00:12:13.483 --> 00:12:15.986
and when I'm champion, I'm gonna
come down and show you I'm wrong."

169
00:12:16.110 --> 00:12:18.238
And then I say, "Guys, I'm gonna be
a great doctor one day"

170
00:12:18.363 --> 00:12:20.741
and I'm gonna be a dentist.
I'm gonna be a great scientist."

171
00:12:20.865 --> 00:12:22.585
"I'm gonna be the President
of the country."

172
00:12:22.700 --> 00:12:26.580
And there are very few people actually
are able to make good of the boasts

173
00:12:26.704 --> 00:12:28.581
and come home and say, "I told you."

174
00:12:36.881 --> 00:12:41.478
I got a call from the lobby.
"I'm Cassius Marcellus Clay."

175
00:12:41.594 --> 00:12:43.972
"I'm the Golden Glove Champion
of Louisville."

176
00:12:44.097 --> 00:12:49.228
"I won the Golden Gloves in Seattle.
I'm going to win the Olympics."

177
00:12:49.352 --> 00:12:53.573
Now, this is '58. So I hold my hand
on the phone and I says,

178
00:12:53.690 --> 00:12:56.614
"Willie, there's some sort of a nut
downstairs wants to talk to us."

179
00:13:03.658 --> 00:13:06.161
It was a great meeting.
Very interesting.

180
00:13:06.286 --> 00:13:08.755
He was a student. Wanted to know
how much fighters train.

181
00:13:08.871 --> 00:13:13.843
What they ate, how many miles they ran.
He wanted to know everything.

182
00:13:17.672 --> 00:13:20.050
When he came back
from the Olympics,

183
00:13:20.174 --> 00:13:23.223
the Louisville Sponsoring Group
sent him to Archie Moore.

184
00:13:24.137 --> 00:13:28.017
Well, that didn't work because, see,
two star qualities don't blend.

185
00:13:28.141 --> 00:13:31.145
In fact, what broke the camel's back,

186
00:13:31.269 --> 00:13:33.818
Archie Moore asked him
to sweep the kitchen.

187
00:13:33.938 --> 00:13:36.657
He said, "I didn't sweep the kitchen
for my mother."

188
00:13:36.774 --> 00:13:38.117
"I ain't gonna sweep no kitchen."

189
00:13:39.319 --> 00:13:41.993
So they called me up,
the Louisville Group,

190
00:13:42.113 --> 00:13:44.286
and they came down
and they interviewed me.

191
00:13:44.407 --> 00:13:46.751
Asked me how I would handle the kid.

192
00:13:46.868 --> 00:13:49.872
And I told 'em I would take my time.
I don't like to rush anything.

193
00:13:49.996 --> 00:13:52.215
Well, they said,
"Well, jeez, he won an Olympic medal."

194
00:13:52.332 --> 00:13:53.800
I said, "That don't mean nothing."

195
00:13:53.916 --> 00:13:56.089
Transition from an amateur to a pro
takes time.

196
00:14:10.641 --> 00:14:14.521
He would come to the gym
and if he didn't get a ride to the gym,

197
00:14:14.645 --> 00:14:17.990
he didn't have no car,
uh, he would run.

198
00:14:18.107 --> 00:14:21.907
He would run across the causeway.
I got a call from the police one day.

199
00:14:22.028 --> 00:14:25.453
"There's a tall, skinny kid running
across the causeway

200
00:14:25.573 --> 00:14:26.995
says he's your fighter."

201
00:14:27.116 --> 00:14:29.585
So I said, "Yes, he is. Cassius Clay."

202
00:14:35.625 --> 00:14:39.380
In the past,
everybody else talked but the fighter.

203
00:14:39.504 --> 00:14:42.132
Now people got to wanna know
the star.

204
00:14:42.256 --> 00:14:45.806
And Life magazine,
the biggest magazine in the world.

205
00:14:45.927 --> 00:14:47.679
They want my pretty face on it.

206
00:14:47.804 --> 00:14:49.772
I was around with this kid
for four years.

207
00:14:49.889 --> 00:14:52.517
They thought I was a mute
because I never put a word in.

208
00:14:52.642 --> 00:14:54.736
"Talk to my guy, please," you know.

209
00:14:54.852 --> 00:14:56.729
When they do interviews with me,

210
00:14:56.854 --> 00:14:59.698
they are not the interviews
that you do with boxers.

211
00:14:59.816 --> 00:15:02.820
Boxers usually don't talk
and all they can tell you is,

212
00:15:02.944 --> 00:15:08.451
"He ran five miles yesterday," or duh,
duh, duh, good left jab, uh, duh, duh.

213
00:15:08.574 --> 00:15:10.694
I'm not that type of Negro,
black man, you understand?

214
00:15:10.785 --> 00:15:11.627
Yes.

215
00:15:11.744 --> 00:15:15.794
See, the questions they ask me
on interviews and in colleges

216
00:15:15.915 --> 00:15:18.259
is the same thing, or more complicated,

217
00:15:18.376 --> 00:15:21.255
than you would ask the Queen of England
or President Nixon

218
00:15:21.379 --> 00:15:22.631
or some senator politician.

219
00:15:24.424 --> 00:15:28.179
He used to jump and bounce
and pull hands down, you know,

220
00:15:28.302 --> 00:15:32.808
so I wasn't making no excuses for it.
I said, "it works."

221
00:15:32.932 --> 00:15:37.904
In fact, one time I was so influenced
by the media, the Doug Jones fight,

222
00:15:38.020 --> 00:15:41.240
I told him in one part of that fight,
"Get your hands up."

223
00:15:41.357 --> 00:15:44.657
And he got the heck kicked out of him.
I said, "Put your hands down."

224
00:15:44.777 --> 00:15:48.281
This is the criteria,
because everybody kept their hands up.

225
00:15:48.406 --> 00:15:51.580
No big man ever moved like that
and this was the key.

226
00:15:51.701 --> 00:15:55.751
He was special. And you're as good as
your talent you work with.

227
00:15:55.872 --> 00:15:58.170
And Muhammad made me shine.

228
00:16:00.168 --> 00:16:02.045
This is BBC Television.

229
00:16:02.170 --> 00:16:03.797
Well, no doubt about the top feature

230
00:16:03.921 --> 00:16:06.424
in tonight's specially lengthened
edition of Sportsview,

231
00:16:06.549 --> 00:16:08.176
because everyone is still talking

232
00:16:08.301 --> 00:16:12.101
about the fight between Cassius Clay
and Henry Cooper.

233
00:16:12.221 --> 00:16:14.519
I knew the English pub were
tremendous.

234
00:16:14.640 --> 00:16:19.396
I knew for a fact they weren't
gonna like him the first time,

235
00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:20.817
'cause he talked too much.

236
00:16:25.610 --> 00:16:27.863
I knew they wouldn't like Muhammad.

237
00:16:27.987 --> 00:16:33.164
When we first went there, we didn't see
nobody. The people didn't like him.

238
00:16:34.452 --> 00:16:40.255
And we're fighting the hero, Henry
Cooper. And he'd gone, "Five. Five."

239
00:16:40.374 --> 00:16:44.424
People didn't go for that,
you know? But he was just having fun.

240
00:16:44.545 --> 00:16:47.845
And Cassius Clay,
the gimmick man to the last,

241
00:16:47.965 --> 00:16:50.468
comes into the ring
with this monster crown on his head

242
00:16:50.593 --> 00:16:52.186
and all the way down to the ringside,

243
00:16:52.303 --> 00:16:55.102
through the rows of people
with umbrellas,

244
00:16:55.223 --> 00:16:58.602
People were throwing things and
trying to knock the crown off his head.

245
00:16:58.726 --> 00:17:01.479
So here we go.
It's the fight of the year.

246
00:17:01.604 --> 00:17:04.574
Clay from the right-hand comer
against Cooper of Britain.

247
00:17:04.690 --> 00:17:09.412
And Clay has said,
"I'll beat him in five." We'll see.

248
00:17:16.953 --> 00:17:20.048
And Cooper's left eye is
a very sorry sight indeed.

249
00:17:20.164 --> 00:17:24.044
And he's teasing Cooper. Round four.

250
00:17:24.168 --> 00:17:27.672
Henry Cooper, you know,
was well loved by everybody.

251
00:17:27.797 --> 00:17:31.267
I can see why.
And, uh, it was a great night.

252
00:17:31.384 --> 00:17:34.479
After all, he hit my guy on the chin.

253
00:17:38.224 --> 00:17:41.649
Dropped him. He slid down the ropes.
It was dramatic.

254
00:17:41.769 --> 00:17:43.737
And thank God the ropes were there.

255
00:17:43.854 --> 00:17:45.948
That was the end
of the fourth round.

256
00:17:46.065 --> 00:17:48.739
And he hit him about two seconds
before the end of the round.

257
00:17:48.859 --> 00:17:51.282
He came back to the comer
and I was chewing him up.

258
00:17:51.404 --> 00:17:53.702
They're working furiously
on him in the comer.

259
00:17:53.823 --> 00:17:57.123
Angelo Dundee, his trainer,
he really is giving him a talking-to.

260
00:17:58.494 --> 00:18:00.872
Cooper's left hook finally scores

261
00:18:00.997 --> 00:18:05.969
and Clay was down two seconds
from the end of the fourth round.

262
00:18:07.211 --> 00:18:08.713
And out they come for round five.

263
00:18:08.838 --> 00:18:11.216
And now he's stepping in,
and I think this is it for Cooper.

264
00:18:11.340 --> 00:18:14.219
The towel has come in
from Cooper's corner.

265
00:18:14.343 --> 00:18:17.267
There was no alternative.
He had to stop the fight.

266
00:18:17.388 --> 00:18:22.485
Inside, Frank Butler asked Clay if
he still thought Henry Cooper was a bum.

267
00:18:22.602 --> 00:18:27.483
He's not a bum. I must admit,
I really underestimated the fella.

268
00:18:27.607 --> 00:18:31.407
Uh, my nose has never bled
until I fought him

269
00:18:31.527 --> 00:18:35.873
and his left hook was as good
or better than the people had predicted.

270
00:18:35.990 --> 00:18:39.164
And I must say he had me on the floor
at the end of the fourth round.

271
00:18:39.285 --> 00:18:40.925
I've never...
I've been on the floor once,

272
00:18:40.995 --> 00:18:45.421
but I wasn't really shook like I was.
And I have to say he's the...

273
00:18:45.541 --> 00:18:49.045
He really should be
the number-two contender next to me.

274
00:18:49.170 --> 00:18:51.093
After all, I'm still the greatest.

275
00:18:51.213 --> 00:18:55.184
But he should have been the number-two
contender. He's the best I've met yet.

276
00:18:55.301 --> 00:18:56.928
Well, you're still a wonderful prophet,

277
00:18:57.053 --> 00:19:00.523
because you said you'd win in
round five and you won in round five.

278
00:19:00.640 --> 00:19:04.611
But don't you think you were just
a little lucky to win in round five,

279
00:19:04.727 --> 00:19:06.650
in view of the fourth round?

280
00:19:06.771 --> 00:19:08.865
Well, uh,
I don't know what you mean by luck.

281
00:19:08.981 --> 00:19:11.655
I told you it would end in five
and that's what happened.

282
00:19:11.776 --> 00:19:15.030
Yes, and you came in the ring
with a crown on your head.

283
00:19:15.154 --> 00:19:17.748
- Is that because...
- Because I am the king.

284
00:19:17.865 --> 00:19:20.960
I understand you have a Queen
of England, but you don't have a king.

285
00:19:23.746 --> 00:19:26.545
The second time we went, though,
we couldn't walk the streets.

286
00:19:26.666 --> 00:19:32.389
So there you go. I mean, then
he wasn't as glib or crazy or anything,

287
00:19:32.505 --> 00:19:36.260
but the people fell in love with him
because they knew it, he was for real.

288
00:19:40.680 --> 00:19:44.480
<i>Maryum, what do people say?
Do they know you're my daughter?</i>

289
00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:45.943
<i>Yes, everybody.</i>

290
00:19:46.060 --> 00:19:48.483
<i>Tell me the truth.
Do they make any cracks?</i>

291
00:19:48.604 --> 00:19:50.402
<i>"You think you're cool
'cause of your daddy."</i>

292
00:19:50.523 --> 00:19:55.450
<i>Yeah, like, if I do something,
they say,</i>

293
00:19:55.569 --> 00:19:59.073
<i>"You think you're big just because
you're Muhammad Ali's daughter: ".</i>

294
00:19:59.198 --> 00:20:03.078
<i>They say that to you?
Who say it? Boys or girls?</i>

295
00:20:03.202 --> 00:20:05.580
- Mostly boys.
- Oh.

296
00:20:06.789 --> 00:20:08.229
I mean, I knew he was famous

297
00:20:08.249 --> 00:20:11.002
as soon as I can walk and talk
and understand words.

298
00:20:11.127 --> 00:20:13.801
You know,
I heard how much people adored him

299
00:20:13.921 --> 00:20:16.595
and, you know, a simple trip
to the convenience store

300
00:20:16.716 --> 00:20:21.347
was 50 people surrounding our car.
He couldn't go anywhere.

301
00:20:21.470 --> 00:20:23.590
And my father loved people,
so he would entertain them

302
00:20:23.681 --> 00:20:27.527
and stay out there and talk to 'em and,
"He's gonna get knocked down in five."

303
00:20:27.643 --> 00:20:30.066
And I'm sitting here looking at him
and I'm looking at the people

304
00:20:30.187 --> 00:20:34.317
and so I knew this is not normal.
You know what I'm saying?

305
00:20:34.442 --> 00:20:38.993
So I knew really, really young, um,
how huge he was,

306
00:20:39.113 --> 00:20:43.539
is when I would see people on television
that I admire, like Tom Jones,

307
00:20:43.659 --> 00:20:47.664
or Philip Wilson or Sly Stone,
Sammy Davis, Jr.,

308
00:20:47.788 --> 00:20:49.868
and then they would be at my house.
I'm like, "Whoa."

309
00:20:49.874 --> 00:20:54.220
You know, "You're famous," you know,
so I knew he was up there.

310
00:20:55.713 --> 00:21:00.264
My dad played kind of the good cop
out of the parents, you know.

311
00:21:00.384 --> 00:21:01.510
You know, it was so funny.

312
00:21:01.635 --> 00:21:05.981
He would, like, if I acted up, he'll go,
"Belinda, go, she needs a spanking."

313
00:21:06.098 --> 00:21:08.897
And then, you know,
my mother give me a little spanking.

314
00:21:09.018 --> 00:21:10.611
And he'll go in your room,
"Are you OK?"

315
00:21:10.728 --> 00:21:12.288
"You want me to get you
some ice cream?"

316
00:21:12.354 --> 00:21:14.322
You know.
He always had to be the nice guy,

317
00:21:14.440 --> 00:21:17.489
but he was just a practical joker.
Loved to joke.

318
00:21:17.610 --> 00:21:20.614
He just always played around,
very loving, very affectionate.

319
00:21:20.738 --> 00:21:21.830
Always kissing your cheeks.

320
00:21:21.947 --> 00:21:24.245
He'd wake you up in the morning
kissing your cheeks.

321
00:21:24.366 --> 00:21:27.290
"Gimme jaws, gimme the nose," you know.
So he was very loving

322
00:21:27.411 --> 00:21:31.336
and you would think a boxer wouldn't be
a teddy bear like that.

323
00:21:31.457 --> 00:21:34.210
I just had fun with my dad.
I always wanted to be with him.

324
00:21:34.335 --> 00:21:35.552
I was like a daddy's girl.

325
00:21:35.669 --> 00:21:38.969
Daddy's gotta leave.
He's going into training, do you hear?

326
00:21:39.089 --> 00:21:42.093
- Daddy's going to fight.
- I want to go with you.

327
00:21:42.218 --> 00:21:45.518
You wanna go with me? You can't.
Can you fight?

328
00:21:46.889 --> 00:21:50.234
Do you fight?
No, you don't fight. You can't go.

329
00:21:50.351 --> 00:21:52.774
- Here you go.
- Yes, I go!

330
00:21:52.895 --> 00:21:56.240
No, Daddy going to get in shape
and you stay at home. You hear?

331
00:21:56.357 --> 00:22:01.955
- I wanna... I wanna go with you.
- You wanna go fight with me? OK.

332
00:22:03.155 --> 00:22:06.580
- Come on, sweetheart.
- The man gonna fight!

333
00:22:06.700 --> 00:22:08.998
- Who gonna fight?
- The man.

334
00:22:09.119 --> 00:22:11.167
The man? Your dad.

335
00:22:15.042 --> 00:22:17.465
Would you please cook me some dinner?

336
00:22:17.586 --> 00:22:21.090
I go to work all day,
I've been out all day

337
00:22:21.215 --> 00:22:25.436
and when all you have to do is to lay
around in this big house and live easy

338
00:22:25.553 --> 00:22:27.851
and at least you can
have my meal on time.

339
00:22:27.972 --> 00:22:31.226
- Woman, please, is the food ready?
- Yes.

340
00:22:31.350 --> 00:22:33.978
All right. Come on.
Let's go eat, May May.

341
00:22:36.397 --> 00:22:39.071
There's one thing you don't mess with
is colored folks' food

342
00:22:39.191 --> 00:22:40.784
and colored folks' money.

343
00:22:42.611 --> 00:22:43.828
Come on. Come on, darlin'.

344
00:22:48.367 --> 00:22:50.916
He loved his children.

345
00:22:51.036 --> 00:22:54.290
He loved his children,
but he was a blessed man

346
00:22:54.415 --> 00:22:58.795
to have Belinda's mom and dad
take care of the kids

347
00:22:58.919 --> 00:23:03.299
when there was such a demand
put on him as being a world man.

348
00:23:03.424 --> 00:23:09.682
He asked to travel here or travel there
so his title, his heavyweight belt...

349
00:23:09.805 --> 00:23:13.059
What was more valuable than that
heavyweight belt

350
00:23:13.183 --> 00:23:17.814
was having his mother-in-law raising
the children, which she did.

351
00:23:17.938 --> 00:23:20.361
And she did a good job.

352
00:23:22.234 --> 00:23:25.329
He used to love
to tape record conversations

353
00:23:25.446 --> 00:23:27.949
and he would always say,
"I'm gonna tape you guys,

354
00:23:28.073 --> 00:23:31.748
and when you're older
you're gonna love this, these tapes."

355
00:23:31.869 --> 00:23:35.339
And, um, he would always do it
so when my parents got divorced,

356
00:23:35.456 --> 00:23:37.709
he would call me. "How you doing?
What are you doing?"

357
00:23:37.833 --> 00:23:39.393
"What do you wanna be
when you grow up?"

358
00:23:39.460 --> 00:23:43.431
He would always tell me time is gonna
fly, you know, I'm gonna be older.

359
00:23:43.547 --> 00:23:45.845
I mean, he <i>was</i> just, like...
he could foresee things

360
00:23:45.966 --> 00:23:47.809
and knew how important
those things would be.

361
00:23:48.719 --> 00:23:50.221
<i>- Maryum?
- Sir?</i>

362
00:23:50.346 --> 00:23:53.896
<i>You're now 11 years old.
Now, do you wanna go to college?</i>

363
00:23:54.016 --> 00:23:56.986
<i>- Yes, sir.
- Now, what do you wanna do there?</i>

364
00:23:57.102 --> 00:24:01.027
<i>Everything Allah made has</i> a <i>purpose.
Trees have a purpose.</i>

365
00:24:01.148 --> 00:24:04.698
<i>- What's the purpose of a cow?
- To give us milk.</i>

366
00:24:04.818 --> 00:24:07.412
<i>OK. What's the purpose of the sun?</i>

367
00:24:07.529 --> 00:24:10.373
- The sun?
- Sun.

368
00:24:10.491 --> 00:24:16.373
<i>- To give us light and heat.
- And make things grow.</i>

369
00:24:16.497 --> 00:24:20.843
<i>So everything God made, the cows,
horses, the moon, stars, ants,</i>

370
00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:22.678
<i>everything has a purpose.</i>

371
00:24:22.795 --> 00:24:25.093
<i>Now, what's your purpose?
You're a human being.</i>

372
00:24:25.214 --> 00:24:28.718
<i>If God made the sun have a purpose,
humans have a purpose too.</i>

373
00:24:28.842 --> 00:24:31.686
<i>Hey, you haven't found
your life purpose yet, have you?</i>

374
00:24:31.804 --> 00:24:32.804
Yes.

375
00:24:32.805 --> 00:24:36.435
<i>If everybodyb born for a purpose,
what do you think you were born for?</i>

376
00:24:36.558 --> 00:24:41.109
<i>To make people feel better.
To fix people up.</i>

377
00:24:41.230 --> 00:24:43.824
<i>That's good. That's good, Maryum.</i>

378
00:24:53.367 --> 00:25:00.251
This bout, 15 rounds for the
heavyweight championship of the world.

379
00:25:00.374 --> 00:25:06.677
The challenger from
Louisville, Kentucky, Cassius Clay.

380
00:25:06.797 --> 00:25:10.802
And his opponent from Denver, Colorado,

381
00:25:10.926 --> 00:25:18.856
the heavyweight champion of the world,
Charles Sonny Liston.

382
00:25:18.976 --> 00:25:23.231
Ali, by then,
had had 19 professional fights.

383
00:25:23.355 --> 00:25:26.154
It wasn't as though
he'd suddenly come upon the scene.

384
00:25:26.275 --> 00:25:29.620
He'd been properly, properly managed

385
00:25:29.737 --> 00:25:34.664
in the sense that, uh,
Dundee was an expert match maker.

386
00:25:34.783 --> 00:25:38.458
I mean, match making is
one of the great skills in boxing.

387
00:25:38.579 --> 00:25:40.456
Finding the right guy at the right time.

388
00:25:44.585 --> 00:25:48.260
Sonny Liston was a gangster.
I mean, he was a mob guy.

389
00:25:48.380 --> 00:25:53.602
He had fast hands and he could punch.
You wouldn't take him lightly.

390
00:25:56.638 --> 00:26:01.360
Nobody gave, uh, Ali,
or Clay as he then was, a chance.

391
00:26:01.477 --> 00:26:05.107
Liston will kill him.
Kill him in the first round.

392
00:26:06.398 --> 00:26:08.617
It'll be Liston in three rounds.

393
00:26:08.734 --> 00:26:11.453
- Liston.
- One, Liston.

394
00:26:11.570 --> 00:26:14.164
- Second round, Liston.
- Liston in four.

395
00:26:14.281 --> 00:26:16.033
Liston!

396
00:26:16.158 --> 00:26:19.128
- Well, I am for Liston.
- Who did this guy ever fight?

397
00:26:19.244 --> 00:26:20.244
- Who?
- Yeah.

398
00:26:20.329 --> 00:26:21.581
- Clay?
- Yeah.

399
00:26:21.705 --> 00:26:28.634
I mean, what Ali set out to do was
to get into his head.

400
00:26:28.754 --> 00:26:32.759
And, uh, you know,
it had occurred to Ali,

401
00:26:32.883 --> 00:26:37.684
I think, that, uh,
Liston thought he was crazy.

402
00:26:37.805 --> 00:26:39.352
That Ali was crazy.

403
00:26:41.350 --> 00:26:44.354
- You ain't got a chance!
- Let us see, let us see.

404
00:26:48.023 --> 00:26:50.993
Liston, he can't deal with this.

405
00:26:51.110 --> 00:26:53.829
A fella coming at him with
a sledgehammer, he could deal with it,

406
00:26:53.946 --> 00:26:56.995
but, well, "This fella's crazy,"
you know.

407
00:26:57.116 --> 00:26:58.834
And that's how he went into the ring.

408
00:26:58.951 --> 00:27:00.544
Hey, get back!

409
00:27:05.582 --> 00:27:10.383
I predict that tonight somebody will die
at the ringside from shock.

410
00:27:10.504 --> 00:27:13.974
The world heavyweight boxing
title on the line.

411
00:27:14.091 --> 00:27:17.686
Looking to get Sonny to lunge.

412
00:27:17.803 --> 00:27:19.683
Liston thought he <i>was</i> gonna win
the fight

413
00:27:19.763 --> 00:27:22.733
and his people thought
he was gonna win the fight,

414
00:27:22.850 --> 00:27:24.898
but, um, that wasn't what Ali saw.

415
00:27:25.018 --> 00:27:28.443
Less than one minute more
in this second round.

416
00:27:29.982 --> 00:27:32.781
Another downright hammer shot.
There goes another one!

417
00:27:32.901 --> 00:27:35.404
Sonny wobbles. Got a wobble.

418
00:27:49.501 --> 00:27:52.300
The fight never went
Liston's way.

419
00:27:52.421 --> 00:27:56.096
At the end of the sixth round,
Liston sat on the stool and said,

420
00:27:56.216 --> 00:27:57.843
"That's it, I've finished."

421
00:27:57.968 --> 00:27:59.515
They might be stopping it.

422
00:27:59.636 --> 00:28:01.309
That might be over,
ladies and gentlemen.

423
00:28:01.430 --> 00:28:05.105
Get up there, Joe, get up there.
Get up in the ring.

424
00:28:13.233 --> 00:28:14.325
Get near the microphone.

425
00:28:14.443 --> 00:28:18.573
All you reporters... all you reporters
made it hard on Liston.

426
00:28:18.697 --> 00:28:21.541
Never write about me like that.
Never make me six to one.

427
00:28:21.658 --> 00:28:25.288
You just make me angry.
Never, never make me no underdog,

428
00:28:25.412 --> 00:28:27.585
and never talk
about who's gonna stop me.

429
00:28:27.706 --> 00:28:29.424
Ain't nobody gonna stop me.

430
00:28:29.541 --> 00:28:32.215
No heavyweight in the world's
fast enough to stop me.

431
00:28:32.336 --> 00:28:36.512
- Who's the greatest?
- Who you gonna fight next?

432
00:28:40.010 --> 00:28:45.010
Float like a butterfly,
sting like a bee.

433
00:28:51.188 --> 00:28:54.533
<i>Say, "Float like a butterfly,
sting like a bee. "</i>

434
00:28:57.486 --> 00:29:01.411
<i>- Sting like</i> a <i>fly like</i> a <i>bee.</i>
<i>- Who's your daddy?</i>

435
00:29:04.660 --> 00:29:05.877
<i>Say it. Say it for Daddy.</i>

436
00:29:05.994 --> 00:29:08.338
<i>Float like a bee,
sting like a bee.</i>

437
00:29:08.455 --> 00:29:12.085
<i>- What's your daddy's name?
- Muhammad Ali!</i>

438
00:29:16.838 --> 00:29:19.637
Why do you insist on being called
Muhammad Ali now?

439
00:29:19.758 --> 00:29:21.726
That's the name given to me
by my leading teacher,

440
00:29:21.843 --> 00:29:24.437
the Honorable Elijah Muhammad.
That's my original name.

441
00:29:24.554 --> 00:29:26.754
That's a black man name.
Cassius Clay was my slave name.

442
00:29:26.765 --> 00:29:29.735
- I'm no longer a slave.
- What does it mean?

443
00:29:29.851 --> 00:29:33.697
Muhammad means "worthy of all praises,"
and Ali means "most high."

444
00:29:34.815 --> 00:29:36.375
Do you intend to fight under that name?

445
00:29:36.400 --> 00:29:39.574
Yes, sir. I wanna be called by that
name. I write autographs with that name.

446
00:29:39.695 --> 00:29:41.493
I wanna be known all over the world
as that name.

447
00:29:49.204 --> 00:29:52.208
What attracted you then to Islam
in the first instance?

448
00:29:52.332 --> 00:29:56.257
The Muslim religion is the true
teachings of Elijah Muhammad

449
00:29:56.378 --> 00:29:57.379
right there in America.

450
00:29:57.504 --> 00:30:01.054
No power structure.
Nobody will challenge him.

451
00:30:01.174 --> 00:30:04.769
And the history of ourselves,
the history of our true religion,

452
00:30:04.886 --> 00:30:07.605
our nationality, our names.

453
00:30:07.723 --> 00:30:10.067
See, we don't have our names,
you know.

454
00:30:10.183 --> 00:30:13.813
I notice how proud you all are,
proud of your names.

455
00:30:13.937 --> 00:30:17.111
See, Chinese have names
like Chan, Chong, Lu, Chin.

456
00:30:17.232 --> 00:30:18.950
This is Islamic teachings.

457
00:30:19.067 --> 00:30:22.241
The Russians have names
like Kosygin or Khrushchev.

458
00:30:22.362 --> 00:30:25.992
You have names like O'Connell
or Grady or Kennedy.

459
00:30:26.116 --> 00:30:31.168
And, uh, Africans have names
like Kalumumba, Nkrumah.

460
00:30:31.288 --> 00:30:35.338
And Jews got names
like Weinstein and Goldberg.

461
00:30:35.459 --> 00:30:40.181
And Italians got names like Dundee
and Benvenuti and Marciano.

462
00:30:41.381 --> 00:30:44.681
But we have names like Grady
and Clay and Hawkins and Smith

463
00:30:44.801 --> 00:30:47.850
and Jones and Johnson, but we are black.

464
00:30:47.971 --> 00:30:50.019
- These are the slave names?
- Yeah.

465
00:30:50.140 --> 00:30:52.689
So when I heard this I knew the truth.
It's history.

466
00:30:52.809 --> 00:30:57.565
So Muhammad Ali is a beautiful black
name. The name of our ancestors.

467
00:30:57.689 --> 00:31:01.535
So when I heard this, I just had to walk
out of the church and Christianity

468
00:31:01.651 --> 00:31:03.494
'cause they never taught us
our true knowledge.

469
00:31:07.407 --> 00:31:09.751
<i>You were born
in nineteen-sixty-what?</i>

470
00:31:09.868 --> 00:31:12.246
<i>- Eight.
- 1968.</i>

471
00:31:12.371 --> 00:31:15.466
<i>Daddy won the title of 1964.</i>

472
00:31:16.666 --> 00:31:18.964
<i>That's four years.</i>

473
00:31:19.086 --> 00:31:22.465
<i>Four years after! Won my title,
you were born.</i>

474
00:31:23.423 --> 00:31:25.471
<i>I was in exile at that time, you know.</i>

475
00:31:25.592 --> 00:31:28.015
<i>They took my title because I didn't go
to Vietnam.</i>

476
00:31:28.136 --> 00:31:30.685
<i>- Do you remember that?
- Yes, sir.</i>

477
00:31:30.806 --> 00:31:32.934
<i>You were too small to remember,
but it's history.</i>

478
00:31:33.058 --> 00:31:35.982
<i>- Ah, but I know about it.
- You know about it.</i>

479
00:31:47.739 --> 00:31:49.619
When I was growing up,
he was growing up.

480
00:31:49.699 --> 00:31:54.079
He was younger than me,
but he was a big figure in the Olympics.

481
00:31:54.204 --> 00:31:57.378
He had a tremendous personality.

482
00:31:57.499 --> 00:32:02.005
Uh... and he had an attitude
about being black.

483
00:32:02.129 --> 00:32:07.260
And, uh, that was my game.
That's what I was.

484
00:32:07.384 --> 00:32:09.557
The black man has been brainwashed

485
00:32:09.678 --> 00:32:12.181
and it's time for him to learn something
about hisself.

486
00:32:12.305 --> 00:32:14.808
When you look at television,
you see White Owl Cigars.

487
00:32:14.933 --> 00:32:18.904
White Swan soap. SkinWhite soap.
White Rain hair rinse.

488
00:32:19.020 --> 00:32:23.742
White Tornado floor wax. White floss
toothpaste. You go, uh, he...

489
00:32:23.859 --> 00:32:26.408
They taught him when he was a little boy
that Mary had a little lamb,

490
00:32:26.528 --> 00:32:28.155
his fleece was white as snow.

491
00:32:28.280 --> 00:32:30.578
Then they taught him about Snow White.

492
00:32:30.699 --> 00:32:33.168
Then there's the White House.

493
00:32:34.744 --> 00:32:38.419
When you look at television, you see
these two cars, one black and one white,

494
00:32:38.540 --> 00:32:41.339
and they put a gallon of gas
in each one...

495
00:32:43.545 --> 00:32:46.389
To see which car can go the farthest.

496
00:32:46.506 --> 00:32:50.602
And every time the black car stopped
first and the white car just kept going.

497
00:32:50.719 --> 00:32:52.471
So this brainwashes the negro. See?

498
00:32:52.596 --> 00:32:56.897
I always followed him,
got a chance to know him,

499
00:32:57.017 --> 00:32:59.987
got a chance to work with him.

500
00:33:00.103 --> 00:33:01.525
He was just a part of my life

501
00:33:01.646 --> 00:33:05.617
and, uh, he goes back
as far <i>as</i> my memory goes.

502
00:33:22.626 --> 00:33:26.096
Clay's first taste of military
life, and quite possibly his only one,

503
00:33:26.213 --> 00:33:28.307
was at the induction, or call-up centre,

504
00:33:28.423 --> 00:33:30.300
at the Old Post Office
building in Houston.

505
00:33:30.425 --> 00:33:32.769
Just before lunch. It's all over.

506
00:33:32.886 --> 00:33:35.765
But Clay is not telling what happened
in the private ceremony

507
00:33:35.889 --> 00:33:39.689
where one pace forward would have meant
acceptance of the call-up.

508
00:33:45.023 --> 00:33:50.746
Why should me and other so-called
negroes go 10,000 miles,

509
00:33:50.862 --> 00:33:55.117
uh, away from home here in America
to drop bombs and bullets

510
00:33:55.242 --> 00:34:00.794
on other innocent, uh, brown people
who's never bothered us?

511
00:34:00.914 --> 00:34:03.793
And, uh, I will say directly,

512
00:34:03.917 --> 00:34:11.643
"No, I will not go 10,000 miles
to, uh, help kill innocent people."

513
00:34:16.513 --> 00:34:19.357
Muhammad Ali refused the draft
to go into Vietnam.

514
00:34:19.474 --> 00:34:22.227
He believed it was
an unjust and unholy war.

515
00:34:22.352 --> 00:34:25.572
He said if the Vietnamese came over
and attacked this country,

516
00:34:25.689 --> 00:34:28.613
he would be the first one to defend it.

517
00:34:31.528 --> 00:34:36.785
John Wooten, the Executive Director
of the Black Economic Union,

518
00:34:36.908 --> 00:34:41.334
uh, I called him because I wanted him
to get the top black athletes

519
00:34:41.454 --> 00:34:45.049
to have a meeting with Muhammad Ali
to give him an opportunity

520
00:34:45.166 --> 00:34:47.385
to tell his side of the story,

521
00:34:47.502 --> 00:34:51.757
because there were so many rumors
about, uh, his life

522
00:34:51.881 --> 00:34:54.760
and not going into service
and his religion.

523
00:34:54.884 --> 00:35:00.687
And we felt that if we got the truth
from him and we believed him,

524
00:35:00.807 --> 00:35:04.107
that we would be a body
that would support him.

525
00:35:04.227 --> 00:35:06.400
And I felt he needed a lot of support
at the time

526
00:35:06.521 --> 00:35:09.946
because he was challenging
the United States Government.

527
00:35:15.697 --> 00:35:18.246
Nine top negro athletes meet
with Cassius Clay

528
00:35:18.366 --> 00:35:21.119
to discuss his anti-draft stand.

529
00:35:21.244 --> 00:35:26.375
They include Bill Russell, Lew Alcindor,
and former pro footballer Jimmy Brown.

530
00:35:26.499 --> 00:35:30.879
Uh, everybody had a chance to talk
to him, to question him,

531
00:35:31.004 --> 00:35:33.302
to give their opinions,
to give their thoughts.

532
00:35:33.423 --> 00:35:38.145
And it was a fantastic meeting
because at no time did Ali indicate

533
00:35:38.261 --> 00:35:41.231
that he would go into service
under any circumstances.

534
00:35:42.432 --> 00:35:46.232
Says Brown, the champ is
sincere in his religious beliefs.

535
00:35:46.353 --> 00:35:49.903
He believes in his religion
and his stand is based wholly on that.

536
00:35:50.023 --> 00:35:52.446
Clay's induction refusal cost him
his title

537
00:35:52.567 --> 00:35:55.821
and he faces a possible
five-year prison sentence.

538
00:35:55.945 --> 00:35:59.290
He claims exemption as a minister
of the Black Muslim faith.

539
00:36:00.575 --> 00:36:06.457
Each and every individual there,
uh, admired tremendously

540
00:36:06.581 --> 00:36:10.961
because Muhammad Ali had
become a Muslim.

541
00:36:11.086 --> 00:36:13.680
At the time called a Black Muslim,

542
00:36:13.797 --> 00:36:17.973
and that was
a highly controversial organization.

543
00:36:18.093 --> 00:36:21.188
And anyone that was associated
with them

544
00:36:21.304 --> 00:36:25.434
was going to really feel
the wrath of the system.

545
00:36:25.558 --> 00:36:29.734
The so-called Black Muslims
in America at the time,

546
00:36:29.854 --> 00:36:31.447
Malcolm X, Elijah Muhammad.

547
00:36:33.441 --> 00:36:35.034
It was very, very controversial.

548
00:36:36.695 --> 00:36:42.168
Malcolm X was, um, a charismatic
man. A feared man, by many.

549
00:36:43.243 --> 00:36:47.794
Uh, the power structure
had successfully created, uh,

550
00:36:47.914 --> 00:36:53.421
the image of the American negro as
someone with no confidence, no militancy

551
00:36:53.545 --> 00:36:57.721
and, uh, they had done this
by giving him images of heroes

552
00:36:57.841 --> 00:37:00.515
that weren't truly militant
or confident.

553
00:37:00.635 --> 00:37:04.685
And now here come Cassius, uh,
the exact contrast of everything

554
00:37:04.806 --> 00:37:07.855
that, uh, was representative
of the negro image.

555
00:37:07.976 --> 00:37:10.855
He said he was the greatest.
All of the odds were against him.

556
00:37:10.979 --> 00:37:14.108
He upset the oddsmakers. He won.
He became victorious.

557
00:37:14.232 --> 00:37:16.985
He became the champ.
They knew that as soon as, uh,

558
00:37:17.110 --> 00:37:21.536
if people began to identify with Cassius
and the type of image he was creating,

559
00:37:21.656 --> 00:37:23.696
they were going to have trouble
out of these negroes

560
00:37:23.783 --> 00:37:26.081
because they'd have negroes
walking around the streets

561
00:37:26.202 --> 00:37:27.419
saying, "I'm the greatest."

562
00:38:25.929 --> 00:38:29.934
I could name you 20 activists

563
00:38:30.058 --> 00:38:35.565
that had a part in the change
in this country.

564
00:38:35.688 --> 00:38:39.318
You were living in a state
of discrimination and racism.

565
00:38:39.442 --> 00:38:43.948
And so there were the outstanding
individuals who would risk everything.

566
00:38:44.072 --> 00:38:51.001
The Panthers took up arms. I mean,
it was a time of people standing up

567
00:38:51.120 --> 00:38:54.499
and other people cowering down.

568
00:38:54.624 --> 00:38:57.343
So there were a lot of individuals

569
00:38:57.460 --> 00:39:02.637
that were risking
not only their careers, but their life.

570
00:39:02.757 --> 00:39:05.601
That was a time of...
of social revolution.

571
00:39:07.554 --> 00:39:13.311
We are not seeking to lose our identity
in blood mixing

572
00:39:13.434 --> 00:39:15.402
and our beautiful Black African history.

573
00:39:15.520 --> 00:39:19.445
We don't hate you. We don't hate
those of you who are white.

574
00:39:19.566 --> 00:39:24.322
We just wanna stay black.
We love my color. I just love myself.

575
00:39:24.445 --> 00:39:30.293
I think that, on a personal level,
I've been around him

576
00:39:30.410 --> 00:39:36.383
and there's a couple of things about him
that I think are really beautiful.

577
00:39:36.499 --> 00:39:38.376
First of all, he loves people.

578
00:39:39.502 --> 00:39:44.383
I thought that was a great trait

579
00:39:44.507 --> 00:39:50.310
and he had a way of relating to people
and making them feel good.

580
00:39:50.430 --> 00:39:51.430
And he used to say to me,

581
00:39:51.514 --> 00:39:54.563
"Come on, Jim, let's walk in the
neighborhood and talk to the people."

582
00:39:54.684 --> 00:39:57.187
"Yeah, that's all right,
I'll go with you."

583
00:39:57.312 --> 00:40:02.159
Meet the people, go into the barber
shops. Go into the, uh, hair salons.

584
00:40:02.275 --> 00:40:05.154
And, uh, he didn't dislike white people.

585
00:40:06.905 --> 00:40:10.125
He just didn't like
what a lot of white people stood for,

586
00:40:10.241 --> 00:40:12.585
but when we went to England,
he enjoyed the English.

587
00:40:12.702 --> 00:40:17.048
He would even party in my suite
with the English people,

588
00:40:17.165 --> 00:40:19.008
particularly young ladies.

589
00:40:19.125 --> 00:40:24.598
But, uh... So his heart,
and his compassion for people,

590
00:40:24.714 --> 00:40:30.392
was bigger than just black people
and I think that's important to know.

591
00:40:30.511 --> 00:40:33.139
And then he had a lot of courage.

592
00:40:33.264 --> 00:40:37.269
The risk he had to take and then to be
isolated, to have his crown taken away.

593
00:40:37.393 --> 00:40:41.614
Not be able to make a living.
That was difficult.

594
00:40:43.191 --> 00:40:48.823
And to keep an attitude about himself,
to keep that personality moving

595
00:40:48.947 --> 00:40:51.746
even though inside
he had to be suffering a lot.

596
00:40:51.866 --> 00:40:55.211
Clay now faces a nomadic
existence of uncertain duration

597
00:40:55.328 --> 00:40:59.049
divided between courthouses
and meeting houses all over America.

598
00:40:59.165 --> 00:41:04.342
His occupation's gone. He seems
unlikely to box again for a long time.

599
00:41:04.462 --> 00:41:07.966
Patriots deride him.
The Peace Party applaud him.

600
00:41:08.091 --> 00:41:11.061
His Black Muslim friends keep him
from faltering.

601
00:41:11.177 --> 00:41:13.555
It's doubtful whether he has
the intellectual equipment

602
00:41:13.680 --> 00:41:15.774
to evaluate these pressures,

603
00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:18.564
but under them all he keeps
a dignity and repose

604
00:41:18.685 --> 00:41:22.610
which make it difficult to maintain
that he's either cowardly or dishonest.

605
00:42:02.395 --> 00:42:04.944
I hooked him up
with a fella in New York City,

606
00:42:05.064 --> 00:42:06.862
and he had him doing
the college lecturing

607
00:42:06.983 --> 00:42:09.532
and he went out on the college campuses

608
00:42:09.652 --> 00:42:12.997
and he did the lecturing
and all the colleges loved him.

609
00:42:13.114 --> 00:42:17.415
He was accepted Harvard, Yale,
Princeton, Columbia, Penn.

610
00:42:17.535 --> 00:42:21.256
All of the schools, the bigger schools,
and he was like their hero.

611
00:42:31.424 --> 00:42:35.304
I'm very flattered in coming here,

612
00:42:35.428 --> 00:42:40.650
because you never could have made
me believe years ago when I was a...

613
00:42:40.767 --> 00:42:43.611
When I got out of high school
with a D minus average.

614
00:42:45.563 --> 00:42:48.237
And they gave me the "minus"
because I won the Olympics.

615
00:42:51.360 --> 00:42:54.409
I understand that, uh,
out of people such as y'all

616
00:42:54.530 --> 00:42:57.534
come presidents and governors and mayors

617
00:42:57.658 --> 00:43:02.164
and great doctors and physicians
and scientists and everything.

618
00:43:02.288 --> 00:43:06.338
So I said, "Well, to get something
together, to talk to these people",

619
00:43:06.459 --> 00:43:10.339
it's gotta be pretty heavy."

620
00:43:15.843 --> 00:43:21.065
So I didn't bring no notes with me.

621
00:43:25.103 --> 00:43:26.821
You are happy to have thrown away

622
00:43:26.938 --> 00:43:30.659
perhaps the greatest sports career
since the war for the ideals.

623
00:43:30.775 --> 00:43:32.527
I haven't thrown it away.
I haven't lost it.

624
00:43:32.652 --> 00:43:36.156
I would say I turned it down.
I'm not, uh...

625
00:43:36.280 --> 00:43:38.658
See, the greatest sports title
mean nothing, mister,

626
00:43:38.783 --> 00:43:41.957
if you cannot be free, see?

627
00:43:42.078 --> 00:43:46.458
Boys in Vietnam are throwing away,
you may say, their lives.

628
00:43:46.582 --> 00:43:49.256
I haven't did that much.
I'm still living.

629
00:43:49.377 --> 00:43:53.883
They are dying today to free somebody
they don't know,

630
00:43:54.006 --> 00:43:58.011
so what in the hell is a heavyweight
title and a few stinking dollar bills

631
00:43:58.136 --> 00:43:59.809
for my people's freedom?

632
00:44:33.129 --> 00:44:36.508
I'm an advertising guy, and the,
uh, editor of Esquire, Harold Hayes,

633
00:44:36.632 --> 00:44:41.354
came to me and, uh, was reading about me
being a hotshot art director.

634
00:44:44.348 --> 00:44:47.067
I had seen, uh, footage
of Muhammad fighting.

635
00:44:47.185 --> 00:44:50.109
I said, "Whoa. Whoa, whoa, whoa.
Whoa!"

636
00:44:50.229 --> 00:44:51.856
"What? Whoa. This kid is something."

637
00:44:51.981 --> 00:44:54.404
Yeah! Where's he at'?

638
00:44:54.525 --> 00:44:58.575
I'm the king of the ring!
I'm the biggest thing in history!

639
00:44:59.906 --> 00:45:03.285
A young black kid
with a big mouth, talking sense.

640
00:45:03.409 --> 00:45:06.162
Uh, yeah, it was wonderful back then.

641
00:45:06.287 --> 00:45:09.086
I mean, they were still hanging guys
down in the South.

642
00:45:09.207 --> 00:45:14.555
Ali refused to be drafted
during the Vietnam War,

643
00:45:14.670 --> 00:45:15.512
and because of that

644
00:45:15.630 --> 00:45:20.932
he lost the very best boxing years
he had during that time

645
00:45:21.052 --> 00:45:23.475
because boxers don't last forever.

646
00:45:24.889 --> 00:45:28.644
And so Lois,
who was the cover art director

647
00:45:28.768 --> 00:45:35.572
of Esquire magazine at the time, said,
"Why don't we do him as a martyr?"

648
00:45:35.691 --> 00:45:37.568
I said to Harold Hayes, the editor,
I said,

649
00:45:37.693 --> 00:45:40.697
"I think Muhammad Ali's a great man.
How about you?"

650
00:45:40.821 --> 00:45:44.200
He said, "Yeah, I think so."
So he said, "What do you wanna do?"

651
00:45:44.325 --> 00:45:46.453
"I'm gonna have him posing
as Saint Sebastian."

652
00:45:46.577 --> 00:45:48.579
"A martyr to his country,
a martyr to his religion,

653
00:45:48.704 --> 00:45:50.798
as a martyr to what he thinks
about life."

654
00:45:52.500 --> 00:45:56.676
"Wow." I call up Muhammad
and so he comes to New York

655
00:45:56.796 --> 00:46:01.176
and, uh, I show him a postcard
of a Saint Sebastian painting

656
00:46:01.300 --> 00:46:05.555
where the body was in repose,
but his head was in torment.

657
00:46:05.680 --> 00:46:08.854
And I said, "Muhammad, I want you
to pose like this. I want you to..."

658
00:46:08.975 --> 00:46:14.653
And he looks at it and he says,
"Hey, George, this cat's a Christian."

659
00:46:16.357 --> 00:46:18.155
I said, "Holy Moses,
you're right, Muhammad."

660
00:46:18.276 --> 00:46:22.656
"Yeah, yeah, Saint Sebastian."

661
00:46:22.780 --> 00:46:24.327
And I realized very quickly

662
00:46:24.448 --> 00:46:28.373
he was saying a Muslim
can't pose as a Christian.

663
00:46:29.996 --> 00:46:33.921
Whoa. Gotta do this cover.
What, what...

664
00:46:35.418 --> 00:46:37.546
"I can't do it." "What can we do?"

665
00:46:37.670 --> 00:46:42.346
"Can we call Elijah Muhammad?"
"I guess so."

666
00:46:42.466 --> 00:46:44.935
Have a little bit of a conversation,
puts me on the phone

667
00:46:45.052 --> 00:46:50.354
and I had, like, a 15-minute talk
about religion and symbolism

668
00:46:50.474 --> 00:46:56.481
and image making, etc, etc,
with Elijah Muhammad.

669
00:46:56.605 --> 00:46:59.575
It was a funny conversation.
I wish I had a tape of it.

670
00:46:59.692 --> 00:47:02.036
And, finally, Elijah Muhammad said
to me, "Young man,"

671
00:47:02.153 --> 00:47:08.502
and I was a young man then, uh, "I think
it would make a terrific image."

672
00:47:18.419 --> 00:47:21.719
It took a long time
because the arrows kept falling down

673
00:47:21.839 --> 00:47:26.436
and Ali had to stand still,
which is not his style.

674
00:47:27.553 --> 00:47:29.647
But he <i>was</i> very cooperative,
very helpful.

675
00:47:29.764 --> 00:47:32.768
We made funny stories
and kept him amused.

676
00:47:34.560 --> 00:47:40.909
He's posing, hands behind his back,
head in pain, head in anguish.

677
00:47:42.693 --> 00:47:44.536
Beautiful image.

678
00:47:44.653 --> 00:47:49.375
And we're starting to shoot, trying to
photograph, and he said, "Hey, George."

679
00:47:49.492 --> 00:47:51.586
I said, "What, Muhammad?"
"Hey, George."

680
00:47:51.702 --> 00:47:55.548
I said, "Muhammad, what?
Pose. Do your job. Come on."

681
00:47:55.664 --> 00:47:59.339
"Hey, George." When he does three
"Hey, George"s, you've gotta...

682
00:47:59.460 --> 00:48:02.509
He wants to say something. "What?"

683
00:48:02.630 --> 00:48:07.181
And slowly but surely, he pointed
to each of the arrows and he said,

684
00:48:07.301 --> 00:48:12.273
"Lyndon Johnson, General Westmoreland,
Robert McNamara,

685
00:48:12.390 --> 00:48:17.817
Dean Rusk, Carl Clifford,
Hubert Humphrey."

686
00:48:17.937 --> 00:48:20.065
He pointed to six tonnentors

687
00:48:20.189 --> 00:48:23.113
that had fired the arrows
that were out to get him.

688
00:48:23.234 --> 00:48:27.956
I was stunned how bright it was,
how smart.

689
00:48:28.072 --> 00:48:30.825
How almost poignant.

690
00:48:30.950 --> 00:48:37.253
When the cover came out, it absolutely
blew the minds of millions of people.

691
00:48:37.373 --> 00:48:39.375
I mean, I've had
hundreds of people come up to me

692
00:48:39.500 --> 00:48:42.595
and talk to me
about what that cover did to them.

693
00:48:42.711 --> 00:48:46.807
If you didn't wanna fight in the war,
you were a coward or you were a traitor.

694
00:48:46.924 --> 00:48:53.102
And... or you were a nerd
or you were fearful, etc.

695
00:48:53.222 --> 00:48:57.477
And along... and along comes
the most manly person,

696
00:48:57.601 --> 00:48:59.478
forget about fighter, that ever lived

697
00:48:59.603 --> 00:49:03.949
and he became an incredible symbol
specifically to young people, you know.

698
00:49:04.066 --> 00:49:07.661
So I'm proud of it for,
not only for helping Muhammad,

699
00:49:07.778 --> 00:49:10.281
but helping to fight against the war.

700
00:49:24.795 --> 00:49:27.048
Finally he got the license back.

701
00:49:27.173 --> 00:49:30.143
He said, "Well, maybe we should get
our own training camp."

702
00:49:30.259 --> 00:49:34.890
So that's what we did.
I mean, here's a guy as big as he was.

703
00:49:35.014 --> 00:49:37.214
He didn't have to pay someone
to have to rent their gym.

704
00:49:37.224 --> 00:49:43.197
He had his own place and he designed all
of it. He called it Fighter's Heaven.

705
00:49:54.825 --> 00:49:59.001
These are the little ponies we bought
just for children.

706
00:49:59.121 --> 00:50:03.001
In summertime
when the children come up,

707
00:50:03.125 --> 00:50:06.174
we saddle up ponies
and let 'em ride around on 'em.

708
00:50:07.963 --> 00:50:10.216
- Heckle and Jekyll.
- Heckle and Jekyll?!

709
00:50:10.341 --> 00:50:13.390
Yeah. Heckle and Jekyll, Mutt and Jeff.

710
00:50:13.511 --> 00:50:17.311
Heckle and Jekyll, and Mutt and Jeff.

711
00:50:17.431 --> 00:50:21.186
My wife.
My wife names all these horses.

712
00:50:21.310 --> 00:50:25.235
These cabins and things you see,
we built all this from the ground up.

713
00:50:25.356 --> 00:50:30.704
And I always, as a kid,
wanted to train in a real training camp

714
00:50:30.819 --> 00:50:34.790
with the logs and the rocks
and the trees and the woods.

715
00:50:34.907 --> 00:50:38.832
We had hotel bills, so I built my own
cabins for the fighters to stay in.

716
00:50:38.953 --> 00:50:42.127
Then we had eating bills,
so I built my own kitchen around heres.

717
00:50:48.379 --> 00:50:51.974
His mom and dad would come up
and cook, and his Aunt Coretta.

718
00:50:53.759 --> 00:50:57.138
That was his domain. He loved it.
He loved every part of it.

719
00:50:57.263 --> 00:51:01.894
His brother Rahaman was there.
It was something that he created.

720
00:51:05.980 --> 00:51:09.075
- This is my brother here, Rahaman Ali.
- Hi.

721
00:51:09.191 --> 00:51:13.367
He just... he just got up.
He likes to sleep and eat.

722
00:51:13.487 --> 00:51:15.660
He just got up.
He's come to get a comb.

723
00:51:15.781 --> 00:51:18.284
He works hard,
so it's why he's gotta sleep and eat.

724
00:51:18.409 --> 00:51:21.208
He works so hard. I put 'em all to work.

725
00:51:21.328 --> 00:51:23.205
The comb should be
in the restroom there.

726
00:51:23.330 --> 00:51:26.960
Always open to the public.
All the people would come by there,

727
00:51:27.084 --> 00:51:31.055
and it <i>was</i> really like
the boxing shrine of the world.

728
00:51:31.171 --> 00:51:35.347
He enjoyed it and there <i>was</i> a lot of
great champions came up there to train.

729
00:51:35.467 --> 00:51:37.765
A lot of celebrities came by
to visit there.

730
00:51:43.892 --> 00:51:46.691
I went up to his training camp
and I got in the ring with him.

731
00:51:46.812 --> 00:51:50.237
You know, just for...
Just for fun.

732
00:51:50.357 --> 00:51:52.530
So, um, I said, you know, "Take it easy,
you know",

733
00:51:52.651 --> 00:51:56.872
because how are we gonna get around,
you know, how are we gonna do this?"

734
00:51:56.989 --> 00:52:00.459
"Don't worry about it." He said, "Just
don't worry about it. Just get in."

735
00:52:01.619 --> 00:52:03.166
So then he started to, you know...

736
00:52:04.204 --> 00:52:06.798
And then tried to wind me up
a little bit, you know,

737
00:52:06.915 --> 00:52:10.965
and he said, "Trigger. You called me
Trigger, right? It was Trigger, right?"

738
00:52:11.086 --> 00:52:14.716
I said, "I didn't call you anything."
"Yes, you did," you know.

739
00:52:14.840 --> 00:52:16.558
So we just... So then we started.

740
00:52:16.675 --> 00:52:18.552
So he said, you know,
"Throw something at me."

741
00:52:18.677 --> 00:52:21.601
So I threw a, um, a left jab
and a right cross.

742
00:52:21.722 --> 00:52:26.102
You know, not hard,
because I didn't want him to hit me.

743
00:52:26.226 --> 00:52:28.586
So it was like, you know,
bang, bang and they both connected

744
00:52:28.646 --> 00:52:32.696
and he threw out a left, you know,
just automatically

745
00:52:32.816 --> 00:52:37.743
that, you know, just caught me.
The end of it just caught me.

746
00:52:37.863 --> 00:52:40.707
And I thought,
"Oh, you know, my teeth,"

747
00:52:40.824 --> 00:52:43.703
because I wasn't wearing
any, um, mouthpieces.

748
00:52:43.827 --> 00:52:46.922
You know, it was
just a friendly little thing there.

749
00:52:47.039 --> 00:52:50.919
But just to stand in the ring
with this man and just, you know...

750
00:52:51.043 --> 00:52:53.091
He would telegraph
everything he was throwing at me.

751
00:52:53.212 --> 00:52:56.432
You know, it was only, thank God,
a bit of fun.

752
00:52:56.548 --> 00:53:01.520
And just to block his arms coming
at you, you know,

753
00:53:01.637 --> 00:53:06.393
even though you knew each one
he was just, you know, doing that...

754
00:53:06.517 --> 00:53:07.517
I thought, "Good God."

755
00:53:07.559 --> 00:53:14.113
I mean, to get in there to fight
this man for real must be terrifying.

756
00:53:14.233 --> 00:53:16.327
So then Ali says to me, you know,

757
00:53:16.443 --> 00:53:19.572
"Just, just throw one, you know,
for the..."

758
00:53:19.697 --> 00:53:23.918
So I said, "Oh, OK," you know.
So I went like this and he dropped.

759
00:53:25.494 --> 00:53:28.498
And he's on the, you know,
in the ring on the floor

760
00:53:28.622 --> 00:53:31.626
and, you know, and I'm standing over him
like, like this.

761
00:53:31.750 --> 00:53:35.425
'Cause, you know,
it's just for publicity.

762
00:53:35.546 --> 00:53:38.641
And, apparently,
Gene Kilroy was telling me

763
00:53:38.757 --> 00:53:41.931
that, um, when the photos went
all over the world,

764
00:53:42.052 --> 00:53:48.276
some of Ali's fans were concerned
and said that, um, Ali should stop now.

765
00:53:48.392 --> 00:53:50.815
If Tom Jones can knock him down,
you know what I mean,

766
00:53:50.936 --> 00:53:53.610
then he should stop boxing.

767
00:54:00.863 --> 00:54:03.241
We were in Deer Lake,
Pennsylvania, one time

768
00:54:03.365 --> 00:54:06.369
and there was a little boy there
who looked frail

769
00:54:06.493 --> 00:54:08.996
and he wanted to meet Muhammad Ali.

770
00:54:09.121 --> 00:54:13.126
I said, "No problem,"
and I brought the boy in and his dad.

771
00:54:13.250 --> 00:54:14.877
Muhammad looked at the boy
and he said,

772
00:54:15.002 --> 00:54:19.382
"Why do you have this hot wool hat on?"
He said, "it's so hot out there today."

773
00:54:20.424 --> 00:54:24.520
He said, "I got leukemia and I lost
all my hair. I'm getting this chemo."

774
00:54:24.636 --> 00:54:26.036
And Ali said, "I'll tell you what."

775
00:54:26.096 --> 00:54:29.100
"I'm gonna beat George Foreman
and you're gonna beat leukemia."

776
00:54:30.184 --> 00:54:31.276
The boy looked at him.

777
00:54:31.393 --> 00:54:33.896
He said, "Oh, I hope you're right, Ali,
I hope you're right."

778
00:54:37.566 --> 00:54:41.662
I went and I got my camera and I took
a picture of the little boy and Ali.

779
00:54:43.530 --> 00:54:48.081
And I got the father's address.
I had Ali write on the picture,

780
00:54:48.202 --> 00:54:52.628
"I am gonna beat George Foreman
and you're gonna beat cancer."

781
00:54:52.748 --> 00:54:54.716
"God bless you, Muhammad Ali."

782
00:54:56.919 --> 00:55:02.426
So about two weeks later I get a call.
It said, the boy's father, he said,

783
00:55:02.549 --> 00:55:06.474
"Jimmy's very sick. He's in the
University of Pennsylvania Hospital."

784
00:55:06.595 --> 00:55:11.567
"He's not gonna make it, but the thrill
of his life was meeting Muhammad Ali."

785
00:55:11.683 --> 00:55:15.313
I said, "Jeez, I'm sorry to hear it.
Is there anything we can do?" "No."

786
00:55:15.437 --> 00:55:18.361
So that next morning
we're doing roadwork,

787
00:55:18.482 --> 00:55:21.861
4:30 in the morning
before the sun is up, when he can run,

788
00:55:21.985 --> 00:55:23.237
and I tell him about the boy.

789
00:55:23.362 --> 00:55:25.785
He said, "OK,
here's what we're gonna do."

790
00:55:25.906 --> 00:55:27.954
"When I get done, my exercise and all,

791
00:55:28.075 --> 00:55:30.203
we take a shower,
we head down to the hospital."

792
00:55:32.204 --> 00:55:34.502
So we go down to the University
of Pennsylvania Hospital,

793
00:55:34.623 --> 00:55:36.876
a two-hour ride, and we went in.

794
00:55:37.000 --> 00:55:39.298
Here's the little boy
with a white sheet.

795
00:55:39.419 --> 00:55:43.174
White kid, no hair, big blue eyes.

796
00:55:43.298 --> 00:55:46.142
And he said, "Muhammad,
I knew you would come."

797
00:55:46.260 --> 00:55:49.013
And Muhammad reached
and he held the little boy.

798
00:55:49.137 --> 00:55:54.189
He said, "Remember, I told you
that you are gonna beat cancer"

799
00:55:54.309 --> 00:55:58.405
and I'm gonna beat George Foreman
and that's the way it's gonna be."

800
00:55:58.522 --> 00:56:00.365
And the little boy said, "No, Muhammad",

801
00:56:00.482 --> 00:56:04.737
I'm gonna meet God
and I'm gonna tell Him that I know you."

802
00:56:08.448 --> 00:56:11.292
There wasn't a word said
in the two-hour ride going back.

803
00:56:17.291 --> 00:56:19.293
About a week later, the little boy died.

804
00:56:19.418 --> 00:56:23.173
The father called me and Ali said
he didn't want to go to the funeral.

805
00:56:23.297 --> 00:56:27.143
It was too sad.
So I went over to the funeral

806
00:56:27.259 --> 00:56:33.437
and in the casket, they had the boy
laid out and they had the picture there.

807
00:56:33.557 --> 00:56:36.857
"I'm gonna beat George Foreman,
you're gonna beat cancer."

808
00:56:39.229 --> 00:56:44.281
The boy was gonna go to Heaven and say
he was a friend of Muhammad Ali's

809
00:56:44.401 --> 00:56:46.529
to get a better seat or a better place.

810
00:56:46.653 --> 00:56:48.997
That's... that's a great compliment,
isn't it?

811
00:56:59.666 --> 00:57:01.043
<i>- Muhammad?
- What?</i>

812
00:57:01.168 --> 00:57:04.843
<i>- I bet you don't know your ABCs.
- I do!</i>

813
00:57:04.963 --> 00:57:06.683
- No, you don't.
- Yes, <i>I</i> do!

814
00:57:06.798 --> 00:57:07.970
<i>Let me hear you.</i>

815
00:57:08.091 --> 00:57:11.766
<i>A, B, C, D, E, F,</i> G.

816
00:57:11.887 --> 00:57:15.312
<i>H, I,J, K,L, M,N, O,P.</i>

817
00:57:15.432 --> 00:57:18.561
<i>Q, R, S, T, U, V.</i>

818
00:57:18.685 --> 00:57:22.110
<i>W, X, Y and Z.</i>

819
00:57:22.230 --> 00:57:25.655
<i>Now I know my ABCs.</i>

820
00:57:25.776 --> 00:57:28.871
<i>Won'! You come and play with me?</i>

821
00:57:28.987 --> 00:57:32.332
<i>Hey, hey. Ooh, I love you,
Muhammad.</i>

822
00:57:32.449 --> 00:57:37.956
<i>I just love you so much. I think
about you every day. I miss you.</i>

823
00:57:43.585 --> 00:57:47.180
It doesn't come to me
as him being famous.

824
00:57:47.297 --> 00:57:50.767
He's being Dad.
It really didn't faze me any

825
00:57:50.884 --> 00:57:55.105
because I feel like everybody else,
like Dad is Dad.

826
00:57:55.222 --> 00:57:58.726
I grew up with Rasheda, Jamillah
and Maryum.

827
00:57:58.850 --> 00:58:04.323
We was a close-knit family
and we knew about our other siblings.

828
00:58:04.439 --> 00:58:08.489
We would go, like, say we had summer
or winter break out of school,

829
00:58:08.610 --> 00:58:12.205
we would go to California
where he stayed at the Fremont Place

830
00:58:12.322 --> 00:58:15.417
and we would meet up
with all of our other siblings.

831
00:58:15.534 --> 00:58:18.913
We had fun.
We'd swim in pool, bike ride.

832
00:58:19.037 --> 00:58:22.416
He used to always do magic tricks
and stuff.

833
00:58:26.253 --> 00:58:31.009
I had a bit of a problem in school
because a lot of kids wanted to see

834
00:58:31.133 --> 00:58:34.512
if I could fight like my father,
and I really didn't wanna do so.

835
00:58:34.636 --> 00:58:37.139
So they would pick on me a lot.

836
00:58:37.264 --> 00:58:42.361
I never really wanted to be in the
limelight. It's really no life for me.

837
00:58:44.021 --> 00:58:46.945
I just wanted to be a normal child.

838
00:58:49.276 --> 00:58:53.702
He actually said to me,
"I don't want you to be like me."

839
00:58:53.822 --> 00:58:55.369
"I want you to be better than me."

840
00:58:55.490 --> 00:58:59.336
So how can you be better
than the greatest, huh?

841
00:59:03.915 --> 00:59:05.167
The man's in trouble.

842
00:59:06.543 --> 00:59:10.673
Listen, George Foreman.
People are afraid of George Foreman.

843
00:59:10.797 --> 00:59:13.676
They talk about how hard he hits.
The world has been deceived.

844
00:59:13.800 --> 00:59:17.850
You listen to me now. I've never told
you wrong. The man don't hit hard.

845
00:59:17.971 --> 00:59:21.566
This will be the biggest upset
since Sonny Liston

846
00:59:21.683 --> 00:59:27.031
and I think it is befitting that I go
out of boxing just like I came in,

847
00:59:27.147 --> 00:59:31.823
defeating a big, bad monster
that nobody could destroy.

848
00:59:31.943 --> 00:59:35.038
A hard punch. I'm the underdog.

849
00:59:35.155 --> 00:59:38.409
If he hits me, I'm in trouble,
like the Sonny Liston fight,

850
00:59:38.533 --> 00:59:42.288
but I came back
and I shook the world and I got Liston.

851
00:59:42.412 --> 00:59:44.540
Now it's ten years since Sonny Liston.

852
00:59:44.664 --> 00:59:48.965
I'm meeting another big, bad, strong
monster, knock-out artist.

853
00:59:49.086 --> 00:59:52.716
He's a bully. He's slow.
He has no skill, no footwork.

854
00:59:52.839 --> 00:59:55.888
He's awkward
and I have given him a name.

855
00:59:56.009 --> 01:00:00.014
I named Floyd Patterson The Rabbit.
I named Sonny Liston The Bear.

856
01:00:00.138 --> 01:00:03.733
And he shall be known officially
<i>as</i> The Mummy.

857
01:00:05.227 --> 01:00:09.152
- The Mummy. Why?
- Why The Mummy?

858
01:00:09.272 --> 01:00:12.902
Because he fights, when he's fighting,
if you ever watch him in the ring,

859
01:00:13.026 --> 01:00:16.496
he, he, he, he drags like that after
his opponent.

860
01:00:21.660 --> 01:00:24.413
And how's The Mummy gonna
catch me?

861
01:00:24.538 --> 01:00:27.257
When you're fighting The Mummy,
you just keep a step ahead of The Mummy.

862
01:00:27.374 --> 01:00:28.374
- Yeah, yeah.
- See?

863
01:00:28.375 --> 01:00:29.672
Yeah, just move on, The Mummy.

864
01:00:29.793 --> 01:00:32.216
"No, Mummy, I'm over here.
No, Mummy, I'm over here."

865
01:00:32.337 --> 01:00:35.637
Yeah, no, you're moving too fast.
The Mummy don't move that fast.

866
01:00:37.175 --> 01:00:39.303
I remember the day we left
Deer Lake.

867
01:00:39.427 --> 01:00:41.429
We were going to Zaire, Africa.

868
01:00:41.555 --> 01:00:45.310
Before we left the camp, and Ali ran in
and he called Cus D'Amato.

869
01:00:45.433 --> 01:00:47.731
He said, "Gus, how do I fight this guy?"

870
01:00:47.853 --> 01:00:50.902
Cus said,
"He is a bully-type individual."

871
01:00:51.022 --> 01:00:57.621
"Your first punch must be with... with
bad intentions, devastating tenacity."

872
01:00:57.737 --> 01:01:01.332
I got on and I said, "Gus, look what
George Foreman did to Kenny Norton."

873
01:01:01.449 --> 01:01:06.080
"Look what he did to Joe Frazier."
And Cus yelled. He was in New York.

874
01:01:06.204 --> 01:01:09.174
I could have heard him from New York
to Deer Lake, Pennsylvania

875
01:01:09.291 --> 01:01:10.338
without the phone.

876
01:01:10.458 --> 01:01:13.177
He said, "But they're not Ali!"

877
01:01:16.882 --> 01:01:21.137
We flew Air Zaire.
All black people, all black pilots.

878
01:01:21.261 --> 01:01:23.980
And on the way over Ali was saying
to them he wanted to meet the pilots.

879
01:01:24.097 --> 01:01:27.146
"How old are you? 23? 27?"

880
01:01:27.267 --> 01:01:30.362
Ali said, "See, if black people get
the chance to do it",

881
01:01:30.478 --> 01:01:33.106
they could do something in life."
He believed it.

882
01:01:33.231 --> 01:01:37.953
And all of a sudden we hit some
turbulence and Ali got a little scared.

883
01:01:38.069 --> 01:01:41.915
He said, "I hope those young pilots
are as smart as the old pilots."

884
01:01:42.032 --> 01:01:46.629
He talked about fighting George
Foreman. "Cus is right, he's a bully."

885
01:01:46.745 --> 01:01:49.248
"I know just what to do.
I know how to fight him."

886
01:02:07.057 --> 01:02:09.560
As we were walking off
the airplane, Ali turned to me.

887
01:02:09.684 --> 01:02:11.607
He said, "Who don't they like here?"

888
01:02:11.728 --> 01:02:14.322
I said, "I guess they don't like
the white people."

889
01:02:14.439 --> 01:02:18.239
"No, no, I can't say
George Foreman's white. Who else?"

890
01:02:18.360 --> 01:02:23.207
I say, "The Belgians," and Ali yelled,
"George Foreman's a Belgian!"

891
01:02:23.323 --> 01:02:27.954
And everybody started yelling,
"Ali, boma y. Ali, boma y!"

892
01:02:28.078 --> 01:02:31.628
And I turned to our interpreter.
I said, "What did they say?"

893
01:02:31.748 --> 01:02:35.924
He said,
"They say, 'Kill him. Kill him."'

894
01:02:36.044 --> 01:02:37.341
Muhammad Ali!

895
01:02:47.973 --> 01:02:49.395
Muhammad Ali!

896
01:02:57.023 --> 01:03:00.197
When George Foreman arrived
with his entourage,

897
01:03:00.318 --> 01:03:05.370
his sparring partners,
here he has one of his loyal companions,

898
01:03:05.490 --> 01:03:09.620
a German Shepherd dog
by the name of Diego.

899
01:03:09.744 --> 01:03:13.465
The Belgians used to put the
German Shepherds on the black people.

900
01:03:13.581 --> 01:03:15.128
I knew we were home free.

901
01:03:24.342 --> 01:03:26.310
It was a blessing that we fought
in Zaire,

902
01:03:26.428 --> 01:03:28.146
because no-one could bother us.

903
01:03:28.263 --> 01:03:31.688
We had time for each other.
You know, we had our own cook there.

904
01:03:31.808 --> 01:03:34.357
We had our own movies.
We had it nice.

905
01:03:37.272 --> 01:03:40.367
Ali loved it when his mom and dad
was there, and his brother.

906
01:03:40.483 --> 01:03:42.611
He wanted to make 'em proud of him.

907
01:03:42.736 --> 01:03:45.990
Show 'em what it was
to really achieve something in life.

908
01:03:48.908 --> 01:03:51.661
Getting back on George Foreman,
why he's not here?

909
01:03:51.786 --> 01:03:54.164
- Never mind about getting back on.
- What you mean, never mind?

910
01:03:54.289 --> 01:03:57.042
Why, he's always ducking.
That makes me mad.

911
01:03:57.167 --> 01:04:01.343
Sonny Liston killed the game and
Floyd Patterson fighting once a year,

912
01:04:01.463 --> 01:04:04.512
and now this man who <i>was</i> at
a press conference the other day.

913
01:04:04.632 --> 01:04:05.474
He wouldn't talk.

914
01:04:05.592 --> 01:04:08.641
He sends this bald-headed man over,
Dick Sadler, to talk for him.

915
01:04:08.762 --> 01:04:12.642
And then, then, then he didn't show up
the other day.

916
01:04:13.767 --> 01:04:15.440
Get off my head.

917
01:04:15.560 --> 01:04:19.690
Listen. I'm gonna float like a butterfly
and sting like a bee.

918
01:04:19.814 --> 01:04:22.693
George can't hit
what his eyes can't see.

919
01:04:22.817 --> 01:04:25.616
Now you see me, now you don't.

920
01:04:25.737 --> 01:04:27.739
You think you will,
but I know you won't.

921
01:04:29.324 --> 01:04:31.952
Thank you, Ali.
Thank you, Angelo Dundee.

922
01:04:32.077 --> 01:04:33.454
Is that all the time I get? OK.

923
01:04:33.578 --> 01:04:36.206
Thank you, Ben Degabula
and Dick Sadler.

924
01:04:36.331 --> 01:04:39.175
Don't do that, champ.
Get away, get away.

925
01:04:39.292 --> 01:04:42.512
Mobutu's right-hand man came in.

926
01:04:42.629 --> 01:04:46.133
Said George Foreman cut his eye,
the fight may be called off.

927
01:04:46.257 --> 01:04:49.932
I say, "Ali, if he leaves this country,
he will never fight you"

928
01:04:50.053 --> 01:04:53.648
because you're not showing any fear,
you're not showing any respect."

929
01:04:53.765 --> 01:04:57.190
So I told him, "Take the passports
so they can't leave."

930
01:04:57.310 --> 01:05:01.360
If he would have left, there would have
never been an Ali-Foreman fight.

931
01:05:01.481 --> 01:05:03.984
The other day, messing around
the gymnasium, he got cut.

932
01:05:04.109 --> 01:05:06.111
He's doing everything
to get out of this fight.

933
01:05:06.236 --> 01:05:08.916
Then he almost had a car accident.
I don't know what's gonna happen.

934
01:05:09.030 --> 01:05:11.909
I just wanna see him that day
in the arena,

935
01:05:12.033 --> 01:05:14.661
because that's one place
he won't be able to duck.

936
01:05:14.786 --> 01:05:17.460
The vibrations are against him.
The planets are against him.

937
01:05:17.580 --> 01:05:20.083
And already,
he's lost the first five rounds.

938
01:05:20.208 --> 01:05:23.007
I'm gonna prove to the world
that I'm still the fastest,

939
01:05:23.128 --> 01:05:24.801
the prettiest, the most classiest,

940
01:05:24.921 --> 01:05:28.221
the most scientific,
the greatest fighter of all time.

941
01:05:32.470 --> 01:05:35.064
The African people loved
Muhammad Ali.

942
01:05:35.181 --> 01:05:37.354
They thought he was a savior.

943
01:05:37.475 --> 01:05:40.820
Muhammad could have said to
the people when we went to the stadium,

944
01:05:40.937 --> 01:05:42.484
"I wanna be your president."

945
01:05:42.605 --> 01:05:46.200
Everybody would have followed him.
They loved him so much.

946
01:05:46.317 --> 01:05:49.696
It was unbelievable,
the power that this man had.

947
01:05:57.370 --> 01:06:03.673
There's a theory in life. It's
said that the strong will beat the weak.

948
01:06:03.793 --> 01:06:06.467
But the brains will beat the strong
every time.

949
01:06:11.593 --> 01:06:15.393
He said, "He's strong,
but he has no stamina."

950
01:06:15.513 --> 01:06:20.895
"He doesn't know what it's like.
Round five. Round six."

951
01:06:21.019 --> 01:06:25.399
"Round seven. Round eight.
I've got him."

952
01:06:35.283 --> 01:06:38.662
And that famous spin
when he was spinning, I told him,

953
01:06:38.786 --> 01:06:40.788
I said, "Why didn't you hit him
on the way down?"

954
01:06:40.914 --> 01:06:42.416
He said, "He had enough."

955
01:06:48.338 --> 01:06:51.387
If he would have spent
as much time in the art,

956
01:06:51.508 --> 01:06:54.853
painting and, you know, or an artist,

957
01:06:54.969 --> 01:06:57.768
taking after his dad who <i>was</i>
in his genes,

958
01:06:57.889 --> 01:07:00.017
he might have been a great artist.

959
01:07:00.141 --> 01:07:02.610
If he'd spent as much time on the books,

960
01:07:02.727 --> 01:07:06.448
he might have been a great lawyer
'cause he relates to people.

961
01:07:06.564 --> 01:07:10.785
Could you imagine him
with a Harvard degree out of law school,

962
01:07:10.902 --> 01:07:16.375
going before a jury? Wow.
Case dismissed.

963
01:07:16.491 --> 01:07:20.291
Oh, my God,
he's won the title back at 32!

964
01:07:29.337 --> 01:07:31.760
The fight with Muhammad Ali was
spectacular for me.

965
01:07:31.881 --> 01:07:35.055
It changed my life because I'd
never lost as a professional,

966
01:07:35.176 --> 01:07:38.680
and I'd intended to win
the easiest fight of my career.

967
01:07:41.558 --> 01:07:45.233
I'd get into the ring with the guy.
I bluffed him, I'd done everything.

968
01:07:45.353 --> 01:07:49.324
Beat him up, hey,
basically for about five or six rounds.

969
01:07:49.440 --> 01:07:50.282
I thought it was easy.

970
01:07:50.400 --> 01:07:53.244
Then about the sixth round
he whispered in my ear,

971
01:07:53.361 --> 01:07:57.491
after I'd hit him in the side,
"That all you got, George?"

972
01:07:57.615 --> 01:07:59.913
And that was about all I had.

973
01:08:06.541 --> 01:08:08.964
It turned into a nightmare then.

974
01:08:13.339 --> 01:08:15.387
Everybody expected me
to win that boxing match.

975
01:08:15.508 --> 01:08:19.308
All the oddsmakers had me ahead
to win by a knockout,

976
01:08:19.429 --> 01:08:20.430
as a matter of fact.

977
01:08:20.555 --> 01:08:23.274
And once I lost that fight,
I was devastated.

978
01:08:26.144 --> 01:08:28.488
<i>Look at that face.
Look at that face.</i>

979
01:08:28.605 --> 01:08:32.610
<i>A man who can scarcely believe
what has happened to him.</i>

980
01:08:41.492 --> 01:08:43.335
I didn't understand losing.

981
01:08:43.453 --> 01:08:46.502
He knew something about that,
so he prepared himself.

982
01:08:46.623 --> 01:08:49.126
He reserved his power and his strength.

983
01:08:49.250 --> 01:08:53.551
I had nothing in the back of my mind
to shield me or protect me.

984
01:08:53.671 --> 01:08:55.344
So I lie in devastation.

985
01:08:55.465 --> 01:08:58.719
George Foreman,
the man who was totally invincible.

986
01:08:58.843 --> 01:09:03.314
Afterl retired from boxing,
well, left boxing in the <i>'70s,</i>

987
01:09:03.431 --> 01:09:05.604
I became a minister at
the Church of the Lord Jesus Christ.

988
01:09:05.725 --> 01:09:09.104
I'm still doing so.
And I realized that, uh...

989
01:09:09.228 --> 01:09:10.855
A reporter asked me,

990
01:09:10.980 --> 01:09:13.950
"What happened in Africa, George?
Surely something happened?"

991
01:09:14.067 --> 01:09:17.947
And I admitted that I'd lost
the boxing match. I said, "I got proof."

992
01:09:18.071 --> 01:09:19.744
From that point on I was able to cope,

993
01:09:19.864 --> 01:09:22.743
because I realized not only did
I lose the boxing match,

994
01:09:22.867 --> 01:09:25.416
but I lost to the greatest man
I've ever met.

995
01:09:25.536 --> 01:09:27.083
Somebody who would never give up.

996
01:09:31.125 --> 01:09:34.095
George Foreman, how are you doing?

997
01:09:34.212 --> 01:09:37.887
Your name, as young as you are,
in my training camp,

998
01:09:38.007 --> 01:09:42.228
me working with you, get your weight
down and you're not fighting for money.

999
01:09:42.345 --> 01:09:45.189
God knows this in your heart.
You ain't gotta answer the man.

1000
01:09:45.306 --> 01:09:48.981
See, man judges man's actions.
God judges man's heart.

1001
01:09:49.102 --> 01:09:53.699
You go in the ring for God,
now you got the whole world shook up.

1002
01:09:53.815 --> 01:09:57.160
"George Foreman came back!" Now
you go out and you start preaching.

1003
01:09:57.276 --> 01:09:59.199
You got thousands of people
coming to you.

1004
01:09:59.320 --> 01:10:01.664
Don't go out a loser.
You go out a winner.

1005
01:10:01.781 --> 01:10:04.660
And this is the wild idea I have.

1006
01:10:04.784 --> 01:10:07.384
Sometimes people come to me
and say, "What do you think?"

1007
01:10:07.412 --> 01:10:09.005
"Was Muhammad Ali the greatest boxer?"

1008
01:10:09.122 --> 01:10:15.050
And I feel almost insulted, because
boxing was just something he did.

1009
01:10:15.169 --> 01:10:18.093
I mean, that's no way to define
Muhammad Ali.

1010
01:10:18.214 --> 01:10:22.720
He was one of the greatest men to ever
appear on the scene of the Earth.

1011
01:10:26.764 --> 01:10:29.062
<i>Let me ask Laila something.
Wait. Y'all be quiet.</i>

1012
01:10:29.183 --> 01:10:31.527
<i>Now, this is a microphone.</i>

1013
01:10:31.644 --> 01:10:33.237
<i>And when you get to be a big girl,</i>

1014
01:10:33.354 --> 01:10:35.527
<i>I'm gonna play this back
so you can hear it.</i>

1015
01:10:35.648 --> 01:10:39.073
<i>Say what I say. Say, "I love my daddy."</i>

1016
01:10:39.193 --> 01:10:42.788
<i>- I love my daddy.</i>
<i>- I love my poo-poo head daddy.</i>

1017
01:10:42.905 --> 01:10:46.079
<i>Hana, OK. Wait, wait, wait.
I'm just gonna ask you a question.</i>

1018
01:10:46.200 --> 01:10:48.874
<i>- What's your mother's name, Hana?
- Veronica.</i>

1019
01:10:48.995 --> 01:10:51.214
<i>"AU". And, Laila,
what's your mother's name?</i>

1020
01:10:51.330 --> 01:10:53.833
<i>- Wonica.
- Whatk her name?</i>

1021
01:10:53.958 --> 01:10:56.757
<i>- Wonica.
- Venvonica?</i>

1022
01:10:59.881 --> 01:11:03.681
I first met Muhammad
when I was 18 years old

1023
01:11:03.801 --> 01:11:06.850
and I was off from college
for the summer.

1024
01:11:06.971 --> 01:11:08.723
I didn't like him.

1025
01:11:08.848 --> 01:11:13.228
The only thing I really knew about him
was that he was a big mouth.

1026
01:11:13.352 --> 01:11:17.607
Um, the boys in high school
in chemistry class

1027
01:11:17.732 --> 01:11:19.655
<i>were taking about the Frazier fight
and 'rust...</i>

1028
01:11:19.776 --> 01:11:24.156
I heard them talking about the
personality, so I made up my mind.

1029
01:11:24.280 --> 01:11:28.581
"Oh, I don't like this Muhammad Ali.
He's, like, a bragger and a big mouth."

1030
01:11:29.786 --> 01:11:33.666
Then we met again in Africa, in Zaire.

1031
01:11:33.790 --> 01:11:39.263
I was helping promote the George
Foreman-Muhammad Ali fight at the time.

1032
01:11:39.378 --> 01:11:43.178
One of his entourage asked
if we wanted to go and meet him

1033
01:11:43.299 --> 01:11:44.596
and visit his training camp,

1034
01:11:44.717 --> 01:11:51.316
so I went with a few other people and he
didn't really pay any attention to me.

1035
01:11:51.432 --> 01:11:57.530
However, when it was time to go back,
we took a bus back, which was fun,

1036
01:11:57.647 --> 01:12:04.246
um, but Muhammad decided he was
going to ride on the bus back with us

1037
01:12:04.362 --> 01:12:08.117
and he ended up sitting with me
and talking to me the whole time.

1038
01:12:08.241 --> 01:12:13.589
My first thought was, "Wow,
he's really nice. He's very quiet."

1039
01:12:13.704 --> 01:12:17.254
Uh, he later told me it was
because he was afraid to say anything.

1040
01:12:17.375 --> 01:12:22.176
He didn't know what to say. But, um,
I thought, "Oh, he's really nice."

1041
01:12:22.296 --> 01:12:26.051
When the fight was postponed
and he kept inviting me back

1042
01:12:26.175 --> 01:12:28.894
and, um, he told me
he was getting divorced.

1043
01:12:29.011 --> 01:12:33.482
He wasn't living with a wife
so I didn't know any better,

1044
01:12:33.599 --> 01:12:36.694
um, at the time, in the very beginning.

1045
01:12:36.811 --> 01:12:42.614
But, um, he would give these lectures
on love and friendship

1046
01:12:42.733 --> 01:12:44.653
and I remember the moment I fell
in love with him

1047
01:12:44.694 --> 01:12:47.038
when he was, uh... gonna make me cry.

1048
01:12:51.659 --> 01:12:53.957
It was just very touching.

1049
01:12:57.373 --> 01:13:01.469
We actually got married, um,
when I was 21.

1050
01:13:01.586 --> 01:13:05.682
And, uh, we had been together already
since I was 18 years old,

1051
01:13:05.798 --> 01:13:09.769
even though we did have
a wedding ceremony in Zaire,

1052
01:13:09.886 --> 01:13:12.560
which lots of people don't realize.

1053
01:13:14.640 --> 01:13:16.563
Living with Muhammad was never dull.

1054
01:13:16.684 --> 01:13:19.938
You never knew what you'd be
coming home to any given day.

1055
01:13:24.317 --> 01:13:29.574
<i>I heard you breathing
and started to talk.</i>

1056
01:13:29.697 --> 01:13:33.827
<i>I could hear your big breath breathing
and so could that lady.</i>

1057
01:13:35.620 --> 01:13:38.965
<i>And don'! You be playing that
in front of everybody.</i>

1058
01:13:39.081 --> 01:13:41.880
<i>I don't want everyone listening to me
talking on the phone.</i>

1059
01:13:42.001 --> 01:13:44.129
<i>- It could be history.
- No!</i>

1060
01:13:44.253 --> 01:13:45.253
<i>Why?</i>

1061
01:13:45.338 --> 01:13:47.386
<i>I'd rather have
something else.</i>

1062
01:13:48.633 --> 01:13:55.266
He took control of his destiny
by making himself accountable, um,

1063
01:13:55.389 --> 01:14:00.361
with things like his predictions where
he would, you know, say what round.

1064
01:14:00.478 --> 01:14:03.573
Then he'd sort of pretty much live
into his future

1065
01:14:03.689 --> 01:14:07.068
and be more, um, pressured, actually

1066
01:14:07.193 --> 01:14:11.118
or accountable to do
what he had said he would do.

1067
01:14:11.238 --> 01:14:17.120
And, uh, so he pretty much,
I think, mastered life in that way.

1068
01:14:19.455 --> 01:14:24.803
<i>- But now Hana's</i> a <i>little girl.</i>
<i>- Now Hana's a little girl.</i>

1069
01:14:24.919 --> 01:14:29.049
<i>- She's three years old.
<i>- She's three years old.</i>

1070
01:14:29.173 --> 01:14:30.971
<i>And one day she'll be a big lady.</i>

1071
01:14:31.092 --> 01:14:34.141
<i>And one day she'll be a big lady</i>

1072
01:14:34.261 --> 01:14:40.894
<i>and grow up a pretty lady
and she never be a little lady no more.</i>

1073
01:14:41.018 --> 01:14:43.862
<i>Right.
And be a pretty lady like Momma.</i>

1074
01:14:43.980 --> 01:14:47.951
<i>And pretty,
and be a pretty lady like Veronica.</i>

1075
01:14:50.903 --> 01:14:53.656
<i>Come here, Hana.
Come here. Come on.</i>

1076
01:14:53.781 --> 01:14:57.331
<i>Each night before you go to bed,
my baby.</i>

1077
01:14:57.451 --> 01:15:02.799
<i>Each night my... go to bed,
my baby.</i>

1078
01:15:02.915 --> 01:15:06.965
<i>While I'm far away from you,
my baby.</i>

1079
01:15:09.797 --> 01:15:14.974
Those were, like, the idyllic,
um, fairy-tale days, I think.

1080
01:15:16.470 --> 01:15:17.938
We were always there,

1081
01:15:18.055 --> 01:15:23.027
whereas the other kids were, um,
in school and that type of thing

1082
01:15:23.144 --> 01:15:28.901
and he, um, he spent the most time
with both my daughters,

1083
01:15:29.025 --> 01:15:31.244
um, from the moment they were born on.

1084
01:15:31.360 --> 01:15:33.320
We were always there.
We went everywhere with him.

1085
01:16:03.017 --> 01:16:08.865
He's a really good-hearted person.
Very sensitive.

1086
01:16:08.981 --> 01:16:14.033
And I guess I'm crying
because of his situation now, you know,

1087
01:16:14.153 --> 01:16:15.450
and I'll always love him.

1088
01:16:15.571 --> 01:16:21.123
It's... I mean, not, like, "in love,"
but, I mean, we've always been friends.

1089
01:16:21.243 --> 01:16:24.793
Um, it became hard to live with him
because of, you know...

1090
01:16:24.914 --> 01:16:30.341
Everyone knows, the whole world knows,
he wasn't faithful as a husband.

1091
01:16:30.461 --> 01:16:33.681
Uh, there's a story to that, too,
I think.

1092
01:16:33.798 --> 01:16:38.554
But, um, he's an incredible human being.

1093
01:16:38.677 --> 01:16:42.727
He has a beautiful heart
and very sensitive.

1094
01:16:42.848 --> 01:16:45.852
I mean, he cries a lot.
People don't know that.

1095
01:16:45.976 --> 01:16:50.322
You know, when something touches him
he'll start crying, more than I do.

1096
01:16:52.316 --> 01:16:55.570
There are lots of people,
actors in movies,

1097
01:16:55.694 --> 01:16:58.322
playing great parts
and that are idolized,

1098
01:16:58.447 --> 01:17:01.917
but it's all a fantasy
and Muhammad was real.

1099
01:17:02.034 --> 01:17:07.712
He stood up for what he believed and
he was 100% sincere about all of that.

1100
01:17:07.832 --> 01:17:13.180
Um, and I think people just feel
the love,

1101
01:17:13.295 --> 01:17:15.468
that genuine love that comes from him.

1102
01:17:15.589 --> 01:17:18.559
It emanates from him
and people can feel that.

1103
01:17:28.227 --> 01:17:32.949
At that time in the '70s you very rarely
see the big stars come to Newcastle.

1104
01:17:33.065 --> 01:17:36.865
Incredibly, Muhammad Ali turned up.
Not only did he come,

1105
01:17:36.986 --> 01:17:41.366
he had just been married a couple
of months before to Veronica Porche

1106
01:17:41.490 --> 01:17:44.039
and they had their wedding blessed
in a local mosque.

1107
01:17:44.160 --> 01:17:46.834
<i>Its</i> a <i>far cry
from fashionable Beverly Hills.</i>

1108
01:17:46.954 --> 01:17:50.925
<i>The mosque comes as a surprise in
the industrial heart of South Shields.</i>

1109
01:17:51.041 --> 01:17:54.762
<i>The children of the traditional Muslim
community here were out in force</i>

1110
01:17:54.879 --> 01:17:57.598
<i>to greet the champion
and his bride, Veronica.</i>

1111
01:18:03.846 --> 01:18:05.723
There were so many things
to say.

1112
01:18:05.848 --> 01:18:08.772
I wanted to tell him
I was his greatest fan of all times.

1113
01:18:08.893 --> 01:18:11.942
I wanted so many things in me heart
and in me head I wanted to tell him.

1114
01:18:12.062 --> 01:18:14.690
He's my hero
and I've got a poem for him.

1115
01:18:14.815 --> 01:18:18.319
My poem goes, "Muhammad,
I welcome you here to my town."

1116
01:18:18.444 --> 01:18:22.199
"You come here with no furious frown,
plus your heavyweight boxing crown."

1117
01:18:22.323 --> 01:18:24.872
"Oh, Great One, oh, Great One,
enjoy your stay."

1118
01:18:24.992 --> 01:18:26.994
So afterwards I used to write to him.

1119
01:18:27.119 --> 01:18:29.622
Writing to him in America,
writing to his training camp,

1120
01:18:29.747 --> 01:18:33.172
and saying the things that I wanted
to say to him when he was in Newcastle.

1121
01:18:33.292 --> 01:18:34.372
And this went on for years.

1122
01:18:44.970 --> 01:18:47.769
"Russell, Russell, Russell!
It's Muhammad Ali on the phone!"

1123
01:18:47.890 --> 01:18:50.690
I thought she was just winding us up
because we were just watching him.

1124
01:18:50.726 --> 01:18:52.478
We're still watching him
on the television.

1125
01:18:52.603 --> 01:18:55.026
And I thought... I just ignore her.
I was still watching.

1126
01:18:55.147 --> 01:18:57.387
She says, "Hurry up!
He'll put the phone down. Hurry up."

1127
01:18:57.483 --> 01:18:59.986
So, you know,
obviously there was something behind it.

1128
01:19:00.110 --> 01:19:02.829
I run into the kitchen.
"Muhammad, is that you, Muhammad?"

1129
01:19:02.947 --> 01:19:05.291
"Yeah, Russ, it's me, Muhammad Ali."

1130
01:19:05.407 --> 01:19:09.037
And it was... it <i>was</i> like...
you couldn't believe it was happening.

1131
01:19:09.161 --> 01:19:11.334
And he says, "I'll give you a date
when you can come over."

1132
01:19:11.455 --> 01:19:13.799
He says, "Russ, you don't need no hotel.
You can..."

1133
01:19:13.916 --> 01:19:15.668
"I have a room for you.
You can stay in me home."

1134
01:19:15.793 --> 01:19:19.764
I was like, uh...
You know, I was, like, stuck for words.

1135
01:19:19.880 --> 01:19:22.349
I goes, "Muhammad, don't say that
if you don't mean it."

1136
01:19:22.466 --> 01:19:24.386
He says, "I wouldn't say it
if I didn't mean it."

1137
01:19:38.899 --> 01:19:40.401
It was fabulous.

1138
01:19:40.526 --> 01:19:43.405
It was surreal, really,
I mean, the house itself.

1139
01:19:43.529 --> 01:19:46.829
<i>If you ever</i> watch <i>the Rocky</i> films,
in <i>Rocky III,</i>

1140
01:19:46.949 --> 01:19:48.997
you see Rocky living
in a big white mansion.

1141
01:19:49.118 --> 01:19:51.416
That was Muhammad Ali's house
in 1984.

1142
01:19:51.537 --> 01:19:55.587
That was the house that he gave me
a room to stay

1143
01:19:55.708 --> 01:19:57.551
and take us out through the days
and going out

1144
01:19:57.668 --> 01:19:59.420
and meeting the people
and meeting the fans.

1145
01:19:59.545 --> 01:20:02.173
And to be there with him and see it
and be on the inside,

1146
01:20:02.298 --> 01:20:05.928
it was so special for me.
Like, a very special, special time.

1147
01:20:06.051 --> 01:20:11.148
The greatest!
Muhammad Ali, assalamu alaikum.

1148
01:20:23.527 --> 01:20:26.076
You look beautiful. I love you. Bye-bye.

1149
01:20:26.196 --> 01:20:28.574
- Ali in three.
- Au revoir, Ali.

1150
01:20:33.787 --> 01:20:37.542
It's very hard for when you
see people who've been in with Muhammad,

1151
01:20:37.666 --> 01:20:41.011
who've been so ingrained with Muhammad.
It cannot leave them.

1152
01:20:41.128 --> 01:20:45.508
I only had a very small piece of being
with Muhammad, but it never left me

1153
01:20:45.632 --> 01:20:48.476
'cause they were personal times
and they were very special.

1154
01:20:48.594 --> 01:20:51.473
Did you... Owl
Did you spend the night yesterday?

1155
01:20:51.597 --> 01:20:53.099
- Yeah.
- Where?

1156
01:20:53.223 --> 01:20:56.352
Dad let us stay at your house
last night.

1157
01:21:04.276 --> 01:21:07.029
Yeah, your daddy let me stay
last night.

1158
01:21:07.154 --> 01:21:09.782
- You slept in her bed?
- No, he stayed somewhere.

1159
01:21:09.907 --> 01:21:14.287
- Mine? A big white bed?
- I don't know whose bed.

1160
01:21:14.411 --> 01:21:18.757
- I don't know.
- The room with the big long doors?

1161
01:21:18.874 --> 01:21:22.048
- I slept on the floor.
- Get out the way!

1162
01:21:22.169 --> 01:21:25.594
Hana, you're mining it.
I want this kinda bike.

1163
01:21:25.714 --> 01:21:27.011
<i>Daddy?</i>

1164
01:21:28.801 --> 01:21:30.269
<i>Daddy?</i>

1165
01:21:30.386 --> 01:21:37.190
<i>Hana, this is 1979.
November the, uh, 12th</i>

1166
01:21:37.309 --> 01:21:39.903
<i>and it's about 8:00pm.</i>

1167
01:21:40.020 --> 01:21:42.489
<i>Me and Veronica was going out
to have dinner.</i>

1168
01:21:42.606 --> 01:21:44.404
<i>- NOW?</i>
- Yeah.

1169
01:21:44.525 --> 01:21:45.367
No!

1170
01:21:45.484 --> 01:21:47.282
<i>Why you don't want us go
and have dinner?</i>

1171
01:21:47.403 --> 01:21:51.124
<i>- I want you to eat here.
- You want me to eat here?</i>

1172
01:21:51.240 --> 01:21:54.870
<i>- I'm going to eat with Mommy.
- I don't want you to.</i>

1173
01:21:54.993 --> 01:21:58.213
<i>- You don't want me to. Why?</i>
<i>- I don't want you to.</i>

1174
01:21:58.330 --> 01:22:02.176
<i>How old are you? How old are you?
Say, "I'm three years old. "</i>

1175
01:22:02.292 --> 01:22:05.136
<i>HANA". "I'm three years old," like that.</i>

1176
01:22:05.254 --> 01:22:07.348
<i>Well, I'm gonna take Momma
to have some dinner.</i>

1177
01:22:07.464 --> 01:22:10.809
<i>- I don't want you to.
- Well, you wanna come with?</i>

1178
01:22:11.969 --> 01:22:15.894
I don't think that I would, uh,
say that my father was very strict.

1179
01:22:16.014 --> 01:22:20.269
He had very strong beliefs and values
and whatnot,

1180
01:22:20.394 --> 01:22:21.941
but he wasn't really too strict.

1181
01:22:22.062 --> 01:22:24.102
I actually remember when I was young
and growing up,

1182
01:22:24.189 --> 01:22:27.443
knowing that I wasn't allowed
to ask my father for something,

1183
01:22:27.568 --> 01:22:31.243
like, it was ice cream after hours
or, you know, Daddy's a "yes" man.

1184
01:22:31.363 --> 01:22:33.043
He was the "yes" man.
Everything was "yes."

1185
01:22:33.157 --> 01:22:35.455
- OK.
<i>- I want to, Daddy.</i>

1186
01:22:35.576 --> 01:22:38.921
<i>- AU". I'll let you go tonight.
- I want to, Daddy.</i>

1187
01:22:39.037 --> 01:22:42.462
<i>OK, you can go tonight.
Just stop saying it, OK?</i>

1188
01:22:42.583 --> 01:22:47.009
Even from that early age,
I realized that his "yes" wasn't enough.

1189
01:22:47.129 --> 01:22:48.972
So I say to him,
"Are you gonna ask Mommy?"

1190
01:22:49.089 --> 01:22:51.329
"I need to hear her tell me too."
So I ran into the room.

1191
01:22:51.341 --> 01:22:54.261
I ask my mother for permission. When
she says "yes," then I know I can go.

1192
01:22:54.303 --> 01:22:58.274
<i>Daddy take you.
I just can't help it. You...</i>

1193
01:22:58.390 --> 01:23:01.769
<i>I want you to ask Mommy,</i> too.

1194
01:23:01.894 --> 01:23:05.023
<i>Yeah, I'll let you go. Right.</i>

1195
01:23:05.147 --> 01:23:07.024
I would not say there was a difference

1196
01:23:07.149 --> 01:23:08.867
between my father publicly
and privately,

1197
01:23:08.984 --> 01:23:11.908
because he's always very charismatic.

1198
01:23:12.029 --> 01:23:15.579
Always looking to entertain,
make you smile, make you laugh.

1199
01:23:15.699 --> 01:23:17.872
It's not something he really turned on.

1200
01:23:17.993 --> 01:23:22.043
But at the same time he would turn it
up a notch, I think, for the crowd.

1201
01:23:22.164 --> 01:23:25.634
But he is always Muhammad Ali,
even at home, you know?

1202
01:23:25.751 --> 01:23:26.751
He was always joking.

1203
01:23:26.793 --> 01:23:29.922
I'd come home from school and he'd be
sitting behind his office desk

1204
01:23:30.047 --> 01:23:35.304
and sometimes he'd have a little wire,
um, arrow going through his head,

1205
01:23:35.427 --> 01:23:39.022
just with a little bit of red blood,
pretending that he was dead.

1206
01:23:39.139 --> 01:23:41.608
And we'd come home and find him
and we're so used to him,

1207
01:23:41.725 --> 01:23:44.524
we just say, "Oh, Daddy,"
and jump in his lap anyway,

1208
01:23:44.645 --> 01:23:46.818
and just didn't even acknowledge it,
you know.

1209
01:23:46.939 --> 01:23:49.067
<i>When I meet Joe Frazier,</i>

1210
01:23:49.191 --> 01:23:53.822
<i>this will be like</i> a <i>good amateur
fighting a real professional.</i>

1211
01:23:53.946 --> 01:23:56.449
<i>This will be like</i> a <i>kid
out of the Olympics</i>

1212
01:23:56.573 --> 01:24:00.498
<i>meeting the fastest heavyweight
champion that ever lived.</i>

1213
01:24:00.619 --> 01:24:01.871
<i>This will be no contest.</i>

1214
01:24:01.995 --> 01:24:04.544
<i>What do you say, Joe?
What do you say to that?</i>

1215
01:24:04.665 --> 01:24:07.043
<i>I say he's nothing
but a bunch of noise, that's all.</i>

1216
01:24:07.167 --> 01:24:10.046
<i>This is the day, man.
You understand? I just want you</i> to...

1217
01:24:10.170 --> 01:24:13.049
<i>You're not fighting Quarry,
you're not fighting Oscar Bonavena,</i>

1218
01:24:13.173 --> 01:24:15.847
<i>you're not fighting Sonny Liston.
You're fighting Joe Frazier.</i>

1219
01:24:15.968 --> 01:24:17.220
<i>Everybody knows that.</i>

1220
01:24:19.596 --> 01:24:23.817
They had a big rivalry, um,
back in the '70s, early '70s.

1221
01:24:23.934 --> 01:24:28.405
It was a pretty heavy thing, you know.
Um, you know, it was rough.

1222
01:24:28.522 --> 01:24:30.274
It was rough.

1223
01:24:30.399 --> 01:24:33.198
Well, I think
that Ali is probably clowning,

1224
01:24:33.318 --> 01:24:38.119
but there is no question in my mind
that Joe Frazier is not clowning.

1225
01:24:38.240 --> 01:24:42.211
They threw off
their respective earpieces, microphones.

1226
01:24:42.327 --> 01:24:43.954
Joe Fraziefs <i>watch</i> came off.

1227
01:24:44.079 --> 01:24:46.298
There was a wrestling bout on the floor

1228
01:24:46.415 --> 01:24:50.716
and we're really very sorry
this happened.

1229
01:24:50.836 --> 01:24:54.215
And there's no question about it.
Joe Frazier is leaving the studio now

1230
01:24:54.339 --> 01:24:57.718
and he is deeply upset
about Ali calling him ignorant.

1231
01:24:57.843 --> 01:24:58.969
I'm sorry, Joe.

1232
01:24:59.094 --> 01:25:01.472
I'm getting sick and tired
of all this mess.

1233
01:25:01.597 --> 01:25:05.101
Joe Frazier,
the heavyweight champion of the world,

1234
01:25:05.225 --> 01:25:08.195
clumsy, ugly, flat-footed Joe Frazier.

1235
01:25:08.312 --> 01:25:10.280
I'll show you what a real champion is.

1236
01:25:10.397 --> 01:25:13.697
Said that he <i>was</i> an Uncle Tom.
Said he was ugly.

1237
01:25:13.817 --> 01:25:16.696
Said he was a gorilla, you know,

1238
01:25:16.820 --> 01:25:20.666
and Pop just said, "Well, I'm going to
do what you say I do."

1239
01:25:20.782 --> 01:25:22.942
"You say I'm a gorilla,
I'm gonna be a gorilla on you."

1240
01:25:25.495 --> 01:25:30.422
As a man, Pop was a...
he was like a little puppy dog, man.

1241
01:25:30.542 --> 01:25:34.638
He was a <i>calm,</i> gentle, kind...

1242
01:25:34.755 --> 01:25:36.883
He was the kind of person that, uh,

1243
01:25:37.007 --> 01:25:41.103
everybody would want to be
their best friend, you know.

1244
01:25:41.219 --> 01:25:43.972
Mom didn't say too much.
She was always calm.

1245
01:25:44.097 --> 01:25:45.724
She was always collected
and she'd, like,

1246
01:25:45.849 --> 01:25:47.897
"Oh, don't worry about it, son.
It'll be all right."

1247
01:25:48.018 --> 01:25:51.568
And I said, "That's not fight, Mom, what
they're saying about Daddy," you know.

1248
01:25:51.688 --> 01:25:54.728
"It's not right what they're saying.
They call him this, they call him that."

1249
01:25:54.733 --> 01:25:57.202
"Don't worry about it, honey.
The Good Lord will work it out."

1250
01:25:59.696 --> 01:26:01.824
I met Mr Ali in New York, man.

1251
01:26:01.948 --> 01:26:05.748
He was like, "Oh, you're Joe's son. How
are you doing, son? How are you doing?"

1252
01:26:05.869 --> 01:26:07.212
"You're looking all right there."

1253
01:26:07.329 --> 01:26:10.833
But, you know, Pop on the other side,
he was like, "I'm coming to get you."

1254
01:26:13.835 --> 01:26:19.387
The heavyweight champion
of the world, Joe Frazier!

1255
01:26:21.343 --> 01:26:23.471
He was more like a fireball

1256
01:26:23.595 --> 01:26:27.475
because of the things
that, uh, Mr Ali was saying to him.

1257
01:26:27.599 --> 01:26:31.103
It really, you know,
got down into his heart

1258
01:26:31.228 --> 01:26:34.573
because here was a guy
that help another brother.

1259
01:26:34.690 --> 01:26:37.534
To help him when everybody was
against him

1260
01:26:37.651 --> 01:26:40.780
because of not going to the war,
you know what I mean?

1261
01:26:40.904 --> 01:26:43.874
And then he was the one to help him
get his license back,

1262
01:26:43.990 --> 01:26:46.960
and then for him to say the things
that he said to him

1263
01:26:47.077 --> 01:26:49.921
was kinda hard for my dad.
He couldn't understand it.

1264
01:26:50.789 --> 01:26:54.464
Hundreds of millions are seeing
this bout around the world.

1265
01:26:54.584 --> 01:26:56.882
A packed house at Madison Square Garden.

1266
01:26:57.003 --> 01:27:00.257
And I believe in that, uh, first
fight, uh, that my dad, he wanted,

1267
01:27:00.382 --> 01:27:03.056
you know, he wanted to get him.
He wanted to get him.

1268
01:27:05.929 --> 01:27:07.681
Why do you think he beat you,
Muhammad?

1269
01:27:07.806 --> 01:27:11.481
Well, the reason that he got
the decision,

1270
01:27:11.601 --> 01:27:16.198
if you looked at my face and his face
after the fight, both eyes were closed.

1271
01:27:16.314 --> 01:27:19.238
His nose is blood, his lips was cut,
his head was swollen

1272
01:27:19.359 --> 01:27:22.238
and he spent one month in the hospital.
And did you all hear it?

1273
01:27:22.362 --> 01:27:23.362
Yeah, surely.

1274
01:27:23.363 --> 01:27:28.244
He spent 30 days in intensive care.
No phone calls, no visitors.

1275
01:27:28.368 --> 01:27:32.498
Now that's a terrible beating when you
have to stay resting for 30 days.

1276
01:27:32.622 --> 01:27:36.172
But he got the decision. But I'm not
complaining. Next time I'll get him.

1277
01:27:37.669 --> 01:27:41.139
I remember, um, being at an autograph
signing and a lot of the, you know,

1278
01:27:41.256 --> 01:27:43.634
different sports figures would be there
signing autographs,

1279
01:27:43.759 --> 01:27:45.181
and Joe Frazier was there at one.

1280
01:27:45.302 --> 01:27:47.662
And my father and him were passing
each other in the hallway

1281
01:27:47.721 --> 01:27:50.645
and I remember my father <i>was</i>
so excited to just see Joe Frazier.

1282
01:27:50.766 --> 01:27:52.286
He started jumping up and down
and say,

1283
01:27:52.392 --> 01:27:54.952
"Come on, Joe, let's play for...
let's show off for the cameras."

1284
01:27:55.020 --> 01:27:56.988
And Joe Frazier just sort of
put his hand up,

1285
01:27:57.105 --> 01:27:59.403
looked at him and kept on walking.

1286
01:27:59.524 --> 01:28:01.902
And I remember thinking,
"Why did he just do that to Daddy?"

1287
01:28:02.027 --> 01:28:05.156
So that's when I learned, you know,
of the pain

1288
01:28:05.280 --> 01:28:09.126
or the, you know, the hostility
or the anger that he still felt.

1289
01:28:09.242 --> 01:28:12.337
That he had not let go of
or forgiven my father for yet.

1290
01:28:16.666 --> 01:28:20.842
My father was told, actually, uh,
about Joe Frazier

1291
01:28:20.962 --> 01:28:22.362
and everything that he went through

1292
01:28:22.464 --> 01:28:26.935
and he read about how his children would
come home from school crying

1293
01:28:27.052 --> 01:28:31.478
and my fatherjust put his head down
silently and started to cry himself.

1294
01:28:31.598 --> 01:28:33.942
It really upset...
it hurt him to know that.

1295
01:28:42.359 --> 01:28:45.909
And I think also a part of him admired
Joe Frazier. He was a great athlete.

1296
01:28:46.029 --> 01:28:48.828
At the time in which Joe Frazier
came into the picture,

1297
01:28:48.949 --> 01:28:51.873
there was a lot of probably different
emotions my father was dealing with

1298
01:28:51.993 --> 01:28:53.495
in taking the stands that he took.

1299
01:28:53.620 --> 01:28:56.624
Then Joe Frazier sort of, in his mind,

1300
01:28:56.748 --> 01:29:01.219
became representative of what he would
say, you know, was going on in the world

1301
01:29:01.336 --> 01:29:03.416
and what was being done wrong
to blacks at that time.

1302
01:29:03.421 --> 01:29:06.425
So when he got in that position,
the new champion,

1303
01:29:06.550 --> 01:29:09.804
and my father never actually lost
that title in the ring.

1304
01:29:09.928 --> 01:29:13.558
Unfortunately for Joe Frazier,
he just sort of became this fixture

1305
01:29:13.682 --> 01:29:15.355
that just represented all that.

1306
01:29:15.475 --> 01:29:19.355
Oh, he definitely had respect
for my father, we know that.

1307
01:29:19.479 --> 01:29:22.323
I know that and I really realized it,

1308
01:29:22.440 --> 01:29:24.863
uh, like,
'cause after the fight in Manila,

1309
01:29:24.985 --> 01:29:26.703
he came to me and he said,

1310
01:29:26.820 --> 01:29:29.744
"You know, hey, all the things
I said about, uh,"

1311
01:29:29.865 --> 01:29:33.745
about the family and your dad, please,
you know,

1312
01:29:33.869 --> 01:29:36.418
"tell your father, you know,
I apologize about that."

1313
01:29:36.538 --> 01:29:39.917
But Pop wanted to hear it from him
for himself, that's all.

1314
01:29:40.041 --> 01:29:44.342
In the end, uh, before,
uh, my father passed away,

1315
01:29:44.462 --> 01:29:48.512
he and Ali came together in love
and unity and oneness of the spirit.

1316
01:29:48.633 --> 01:29:50.635
And, uh, man,
I was so happy to see that.

1317
01:29:50.760 --> 01:29:54.606
They hugged, embraced and, uh,
it was something cool, you know.

1318
01:29:54.723 --> 01:29:57.647
That was fantastic.

1319
01:30:10.864 --> 01:30:11.706
<i>Yeah?</i>

1320
01:30:11.823 --> 01:30:15.248
<i>Uh, there's a story
in today's "Post"</i>

1321
01:30:15.368 --> 01:30:18.417
<i>that you're gonna go up to Deer Lake
and start training again.</i>

1322
01:30:18.538 --> 01:30:19.710
<i>There is?</i>

1323
01:30:19.831 --> 01:30:23.961
<i>Yeah, so what they're saying is
you're gonna come back.</i>

1324
01:30:24.085 --> 01:30:26.884
<i>- That'll shock 'em, won't it?</i>
<i>- Yeah.</i>

1325
01:30:27.005 --> 01:30:28.882
<i>- That'll shock 'em!</i>
<i>- Listen...</i>

1326
01:30:29.007 --> 01:30:32.807
<i>I'll come back for the fourth time.
I'll take my crown for the fourth time.</i>

1327
01:30:32.928 --> 01:30:35.351
<i>I hope it's not true,
between you and me.</i>

1328
01:30:35.472 --> 01:30:36.894
<i>Well...</i>

1329
01:30:37.015 --> 01:30:40.736
<i>Because, uh, you've conquered
all the worlds you could conquer.</i>

1330
01:30:40.852 --> 01:30:43.981
<i>Can you imagine?
Can you imagine four-time champion?</i>

1331
01:30:44.105 --> 01:30:46.358
<i>Ah, but what do you need with it,
Muhammad?</i>

1332
01:30:46.483 --> 01:30:48.406
<i>- Don't need it.</i>
<i>- What do you need with it?</i>

1333
01:30:48.526 --> 01:30:49.948
<i>Just talking. I'm just talking.</i>

1334
01:30:50.070 --> 01:30:52.310
<i>Nobody is ever gonna be
a three-time champion again.</i>

1335
01:30:52.364 --> 01:30:54.662
<i>- I know.</i>
<i>- Nobody. Not in our lifetime.</i>

1336
01:30:54.783 --> 01:30:57.206
<i>- I'm just talking.
- Yeah, yeah.</i>

1337
01:31:06.211 --> 01:31:09.806
Many people wanna know
why am I doing this.

1338
01:31:09.923 --> 01:31:14.804
Champion, why?
I say, "Why? Because it is there."

1339
01:31:16.012 --> 01:31:19.983
Why did we go to the moon?
Because it's there.

1340
01:31:21.267 --> 01:31:25.989
We're not satisfied. Now we're
mapping out a plan to go to Mars.

1341
01:31:26.106 --> 01:31:29.360
Why? Why Mars?
Ain't the moon far enough?

1342
01:31:29.484 --> 01:31:31.157
Mars, because it's there

1343
01:31:31.277 --> 01:31:34.998
and there's the possibility
and a chance of us reaching it.

1344
01:31:35.115 --> 01:31:39.165
Why am I coming back?
Because it's there.

1345
01:31:39.285 --> 01:31:45.167
All right, here it is.
A comeback by Muhammad Ali.

1346
01:31:45.291 --> 01:31:47.635
Drama of the Bahamas is
now about to begin.

1347
01:31:47.752 --> 01:31:49.112
Drama in the Bahamas.

1348
01:31:49.212 --> 01:31:54.639
They say, "No."
We close the doors, you're finished.

1349
01:31:54.759 --> 01:31:57.603
But if the guy they said "No" to
is Muhammad Ali,

1350
01:31:57.721 --> 01:32:00.474
they said "No" to the greatest
of all time.

1351
01:32:02.600 --> 01:32:06.150
I can remember my father's
last fight with Trevor Berbick.

1352
01:32:06.271 --> 01:32:09.650
We were at Deer Lake, his training camp.
He called it Fighter's Heaven.

1353
01:32:09.774 --> 01:32:14.530
And, um, Daddy would run up the hill.
He called it Agony Hill.

1354
01:32:14.654 --> 01:32:19.034
And I would follow behind him
and behind that would be a car

1355
01:32:19.159 --> 01:32:21.161
that would be there
for when he got tired.

1356
01:32:21.286 --> 01:32:26.213
And, um, just riding, riding alongside
with him, jogging up the hill.

1357
01:32:26.332 --> 01:32:29.632
I was probably five, I would think.
Four or five.

1358
01:32:29.753 --> 01:32:32.427
They say I'm old. Do I look old to you?

1359
01:32:42.682 --> 01:32:44.150
I don't know. I'm pretty.

1360
01:32:45.560 --> 01:32:49.656
He did used to say that it was hard
to get up and have to train

1361
01:32:49.773 --> 01:32:50.773
and do those things.

1362
01:32:50.815 --> 01:32:53.855
You know, put your body through that
when you're already comfortable in life.

1363
01:32:53.902 --> 01:32:55.745
You have a big house,
you have the money you need.

1364
01:32:55.862 --> 01:32:58.456
But when you're trying to make it
and you have this hunger

1365
01:32:58.573 --> 01:33:00.573
and this need to prove to yourself
and to the world

1366
01:33:00.617 --> 01:33:02.617
how great and wonderful you are,
it's a lot easier.

1367
01:33:02.660 --> 01:33:05.539
So at that time in his life he was,
what, 38 years old.

1368
01:33:05.663 --> 01:33:08.712
He already had Parkinson's,
although it was undiagnosed.

1369
01:33:08.833 --> 01:33:10.913
People saw, you know,
you could hear it in his voice.

1370
01:33:10.960 --> 01:33:14.760
The slurred speech and the, you know,
the discoordination in his movement.

1371
01:33:14.881 --> 01:33:17.930
Um, but no-one really knew what it was,
so...

1372
01:33:18.968 --> 01:33:21.892
- There it is. It's all over.
- What a fight.

1373
01:33:22.013 --> 01:33:25.187
- A tremendous fight.
- Avery good fight.

1374
01:33:25.308 --> 01:33:29.154
Ali in much better condition
than they thought he would be.

1375
01:33:29.270 --> 01:33:33.025
Everybody here feels now
that Ali really won the fight.

1376
01:33:33.149 --> 01:33:34.275
Berbick is walking around.

1377
01:33:34.400 --> 01:33:37.654
He's been holding his hands up in
the air as though he did win the fight.

1378
01:33:37.779 --> 01:33:39.656
However, there is a look of gloom
in his eyes,

1379
01:33:39.781 --> 01:33:43.752
<i>as</i> though he may have figured
that he did lose it by a decision.

1380
01:33:43.868 --> 01:33:46.087
My father is one of those people

1381
01:33:46.204 --> 01:33:50.425
that would have spent the rest
of his life trying to make a comeback.

1382
01:33:50.542 --> 01:33:54.388
He actually joked about it
probably up till age, I would say, 65.

1383
01:33:54.504 --> 01:33:56.704
"Wouldn't it be something to shake up,
shock the world?"

1384
01:33:56.756 --> 01:33:59.116
"We'll shake up the world.
Come back, take that title back."

1385
01:33:59.175 --> 01:34:06.559
Judge Jay Edson votes 99-94.
Unanimous decision for Trevor Berbick!

1386
01:34:07.892 --> 01:34:09.485
Well, there you have it.

1387
01:34:09.602 --> 01:34:12.321
Trevor Berbick has
the unanimous decision.

1388
01:34:15.567 --> 01:34:21.950
He's always defied impossible odds,
doing the seemingly impossible,

1389
01:34:22.073 --> 01:34:26.044
you know, and proving to the world,
and himself, that he can accomplish it.

1390
01:34:26.161 --> 01:34:29.256
And I think that's part
of what my father needed,

1391
01:34:29.372 --> 01:34:30.999
and it's part of what made him
who he is.

1392
01:34:31.124 --> 01:34:32.216
Had he not done it,

1393
01:34:32.333 --> 01:34:34.853
he wouldn't be the three-time
heavyweight champion of the world.

1394
01:34:34.961 --> 01:34:37.361
So he couldn't say goodbye to boxing,
so obviously, you know,

1395
01:34:37.422 --> 01:34:40.471
ultimately boxing had to say
goodbye for him.

1396
01:34:42.635 --> 01:34:44.933
<i>Boxing will always continue
without me.</i>

1397
01:34:45.054 --> 01:34:48.399
<i>The Concorde, I understand,
is not too economical</i>

1398
01:34:48.516 --> 01:34:50.985
<i>and they're talking
about grounding it.</i>

1399
01:34:51.102 --> 01:34:53.696
<i>Well, if they do,
jet planes will still fly,</i>

1400
01:34:53.813 --> 01:34:56.032
<i>but you just won't have a Concorde.</i>

1401
01:34:56.149 --> 01:35:01.747
<i>So, I was the Concorde of boxing
and the other fighters are jets.</i>

1402
01:35:01.863 --> 01:35:07.211
<i>I was at a higher altitude than
the rest, moving faster than the rest,</i>

1403
01:35:07.327 --> 01:35:11.002
<i>but you'll just have to get used
to riding on jets again.</i>

1404
01:35:11.122 --> 01:35:13.124
<i>You can't ride Concorde anymore.</i>

1405
01:35:35.980 --> 01:35:38.199
We're on our way to meet
Mike Tyson,

1406
01:35:38.316 --> 01:35:42.822
one of the most unique people
on planet Earth.

1407
01:35:47.700 --> 01:35:50.249
One time Muhammad Ali and I were
in New York City

1408
01:35:50.370 --> 01:35:54.295
and we got a call about a reform school.

1409
01:35:54.415 --> 01:35:58.511
No celebrities or athletes ever visit
those kids, and they're good kids.

1410
01:35:58.628 --> 01:36:01.222
And we went out to this reform school

1411
01:36:01.339 --> 01:36:04.388
and then three months later
I got a call from Cus D'Amato

1412
01:36:04.509 --> 01:36:08.230
and he said,
"Gene, lightning struck twice."

1413
01:36:08.346 --> 01:36:10.690
He said, "I got a young kid here"

1414
01:36:10.807 --> 01:36:13.606
who's gonna be the heavyweight
champ of the world some day."

1415
01:36:13.726 --> 01:36:17.731
"He knows you." I said,"He knows me?
How does he know me, Cus?"

1416
01:36:20.942 --> 01:36:25.197
When I saw Muhammad Ali I was at
a reformatory in the Bronx, New York,

1417
01:36:25.321 --> 01:36:27.164
named Spofford.

1418
01:36:27.282 --> 01:36:30.411
And, um, Muhammad Ali came one day
out of the blue

1419
01:36:30.535 --> 01:36:32.287
and someone say, "We have a surprise."

1420
01:36:32.412 --> 01:36:36.508
We saw the movie The Greatest and
then after that Muhammad Ali came in

1421
01:36:36.624 --> 01:36:40.925
and that was just a really, um,
interesting moment in my life.

1422
01:36:41.045 --> 01:36:45.391
I said, "Wow, that's awesome."
You know, "How can I be like that?"

1423
01:36:45.508 --> 01:36:49.058
Got involved with boxing
and, um, I met Cus

1424
01:36:49.178 --> 01:36:52.273
<i>and Gus was very, um,
acquainted with, uh, A“</i>

1425
01:36:53.391 --> 01:36:56.110
and, um, he would talk to him
on the phone.

1426
01:36:56.227 --> 01:36:58.605
You know, he'd call him up,
talk to him.

1427
01:36:58.730 --> 01:37:02.200
Him and Gene'd be talking,
and Gene'd put Ali on the phone.

1428
01:37:02.317 --> 01:37:05.992
And, uh, sometimes I'd go over
to the phone and listen to 'em talk,

1429
01:37:06.112 --> 01:37:10.458
sneak another phone, put it...
listen to what they're talking about.

1430
01:37:10.575 --> 01:37:14.125
And, um, he just...
it was just in my environment.

1431
01:37:14.245 --> 01:37:19.376
Everything was fighting and war,
and the other concept of war

1432
01:37:19.500 --> 01:37:20.547
and fighting and war.

1433
01:37:20.668 --> 01:37:22.386
You know, it's just the whole thing.

1434
01:37:22.503 --> 01:37:27.430
Who would have won
if you fought this man in your prime?

1435
01:37:32.722 --> 01:37:33.814
Him?

1436
01:37:34.766 --> 01:37:36.484
Ali, Ali!

1437
01:37:36.601 --> 01:37:38.729
Ali! Ali!

1438
01:37:41.606 --> 01:37:44.109
Wait. Wait, wait, wait, wait.

1439
01:37:44.233 --> 01:37:46.656
- I was so fast.
- Uh-huh.

1440
01:37:46.778 --> 01:37:48.451
But if he hit me...

1441
01:37:53.159 --> 01:37:56.584
- That's if you catch me.
- Yeah. What would you say, Mike?

1442
01:37:56.704 --> 01:38:00.425
I... by no means... I'm vain.

1443
01:38:00.541 --> 01:38:02.635
I know I'm great,
but can I tell you something?

1444
01:38:02.752 --> 01:38:03.594
Uh-huh.

1445
01:38:03.711 --> 01:38:06.271
In this situation every head must bow,
every tongue must confess.

1446
01:38:06.381 --> 01:38:08.975
This is the greatest of all time.

1447
01:38:11.386 --> 01:38:16.813
You can't really explain, um,
what Muhammad Ali is.

1448
01:38:16.933 --> 01:38:18.901
You know, there's no word really
to explain it

1449
01:38:19.018 --> 01:38:22.192
as far as achievements are concerned.

1450
01:38:22.313 --> 01:38:28.286
And, um, emotional and psychological
achievements and, you know, inspiration

1451
01:38:28.403 --> 01:38:31.998
that, that, um,
had came from Muhammad Ali.

1452
01:38:32.115 --> 01:38:34.618
Um, you just can't imagine that
in the word "great."

1453
01:38:34.742 --> 01:38:36.619
There has to be another word created.

1454
01:38:38.496 --> 01:38:40.590
My father has a lot of sides,

1455
01:38:40.706 --> 01:38:44.256
and some sides are focused on
and some sides aren't.

1456
01:38:44.377 --> 01:38:46.505
And you hear about the fights.

1457
01:38:46.629 --> 01:38:50.600
You hear about, you know,
he's a good person to people,

1458
01:38:50.716 --> 01:38:54.721
but you don't hear about family
that much or what that meant to him.

1459
01:38:54.846 --> 01:38:56.223
You know, he used to always say,

1460
01:38:56.347 --> 01:38:59.476
"When you guys grow up,
I want us all to live on a hill

1461
01:38:59.600 --> 01:39:02.319
and everyone has a house
on the same street."

1462
01:39:02.437 --> 01:39:07.534
I admire him because I have half-sisters
and he had children out of wedlock

1463
01:39:07.650 --> 01:39:12.451
and he would make sure
we all converged in LA to be with him

1464
01:39:12.572 --> 01:39:13.932
and get to get to know each other.

1465
01:39:14.031 --> 01:39:17.376
He wasn't a perfect person,
but I admired how he was with his kids

1466
01:39:17.493 --> 01:39:19.166
and what he wanted for us.

1467
01:39:24.000 --> 01:39:26.970
<i>When you all get to be grown ladies
and get married,</i>

1468
01:39:27.086 --> 01:39:29.180
<i>and we look back
when you were coming up</i>

1469
01:39:29.297 --> 01:39:32.221
<i>"you can say, "I didn't live
with all my sisters and brothers,</i>

1470
01:39:32.341 --> 01:39:34.844
<i>but Daddy let me see 'em,
and we knew 'em."</i>

1471
01:39:34.969 --> 01:39:38.439
<i>"Daddy let us play together."</i>

1472
01:40:28.814 --> 01:40:32.318
Back in the days when Muhammad
and I was young kids, he would say,

1473
01:40:32.443 --> 01:40:36.323
"Rudy, I can see it in the stars.
God is talking to me."

1474
01:40:36.447 --> 01:40:39.075
He would tell me his destiny,
how great he would be.

1475
01:40:39.200 --> 01:40:41.498
He said, "And I want you to be with me.
I love you, my brother."

1476
01:40:41.619 --> 01:40:43.121
He's a sweet, sweet, sweet person.

1477
01:40:44.914 --> 01:40:47.588
God blessed him with having insight
to predict the future.

1478
01:40:47.708 --> 01:40:50.388
"I'm gonna be the world's greatest
boxer. I'm gonna be a great man."

1479
01:40:50.503 --> 01:40:53.052
He wanted to become famous
to help people.

1480
01:40:53.172 --> 01:40:54.890
He's a wonderful, wonderful...

1481
01:41:00.471 --> 01:41:03.600
I can remember feeling very proud
of my father

1482
01:41:03.724 --> 01:41:10.107
and just an overwhelming sense of pride.
I guess it's a euphoric feeling.

1483
01:41:10.231 --> 01:41:12.154
You know, when I was...
From an early age,

1484
01:41:12.275 --> 01:41:15.529
anywhere we'd go together,
because not just him getting attention,

1485
01:41:15.653 --> 01:41:18.657
but the way that people would react
to him and sometimes they'd be in tears.

1486
01:41:18.781 --> 01:41:21.034
And I know my mother hadn't seen my dad,

1487
01:41:21.158 --> 01:41:24.412
maybe, I would say,
three or four years after the marriage.

1488
01:41:24.537 --> 01:41:27.097
You know, he would come around
and three years might have gone by

1489
01:41:27.123 --> 01:41:29.171
where she hadn't seen him
and when he came to the house,

1490
01:41:29.292 --> 01:41:31.420
we lived in the Venice Canals,
she was remarried

1491
01:41:31.544 --> 01:41:33.797
and she looked at him
and she hugged him.

1492
01:41:33.921 --> 01:41:35.721
Then she started to cry
and she left the room.

1493
01:41:35.840 --> 01:41:37.888
My father looked at me and he says,
"Why is she crying?"

1494
01:41:38.009 --> 01:41:41.229
So I had to ask her. She said, "Well,
I looked into his eyes and I saw God."

1495
01:41:41.345 --> 01:41:44.690
So, you know, and I said, "Oh."

1496
01:41:44.807 --> 01:41:46.525
"I know what you mean," you know?

1497
01:41:46.642 --> 01:41:47.859
He has this twinkle in his eye

1498
01:41:47.977 --> 01:41:50.571
and he has this spirit within him
that's so profound

1499
01:41:50.688 --> 01:41:53.288
that people sometimes are moved
to just silence when they see him.

1500
01:41:56.819 --> 01:41:59.117
It just makes you more, I think,

1501
01:41:59.238 --> 01:42:04.244
cognizant of just the spiritual side
of fame and celebrity.

1502
01:42:04.368 --> 01:42:07.463
Not just famous for being famous,
but it makes you wanna know why.

1503
01:42:07.580 --> 01:42:09.140
Why he was famous, why people love him,

1504
01:42:09.206 --> 01:42:11.800
the stands that he took,
the controversy.

1505
01:42:11.917 --> 01:42:14.261
Everything that he went through,
it's all part of his story

1506
01:42:14.378 --> 01:42:15.578
of getting to where he is now.

1507
01:42:15.588 --> 01:42:18.091
You know, the ups and the downs
that all makes him who he is.

1508
01:42:21.552 --> 01:42:24.146
<i>I'm with him
in his training camp at Deer Lake.</i>

1509
01:42:24.263 --> 01:42:27.062
Cold. Freezing cold.

1510
01:42:28.309 --> 01:42:34.487
Um, you know, ten degrees outside and
maybe 20 degrees below zero, wind chill.

1511
01:42:34.607 --> 01:42:37.235
And he'd say, "Hey, George,
let's go for a walk."

1512
01:42:37.360 --> 01:42:41.206
I said, "What, are you crazy?"
"Hey, George, let's go for a walk!"

1513
01:42:41.322 --> 01:42:45.043
I said, "What..."
"Hey, George, let's go for a walk!"

1514
